f we set u=2 and v=5, the operation yields d=5, which is the maximum possible.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n7 5 20\r\n10 11\r\n4 5\r\n10 12\r\n1 2\r\n1 5\r\n5 6\r\n2 4\r\n3 5\r\n9 10\r\n2 5\r\n1 4\r\n11 12\r\n9 12\r\n8 9\r\n5 7\r\n3 7\r\n3 6\r\n3 4\r\n8 12\r\n9 11\n\nSample Output 2\n\n4",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We are given a graph with $ N_1 + N_2 $ vertices and some edges. The graph consists of two fully connected components:\n\n- The first $ N_1 $ vertices (vertices $ 1 $ to $ N_1 $) form one connected subgraph.\n- The next $ N_2 $ vertices (vertices $ N_1+1 $ to $ N_1+N_2 $) form another connected subgraph.\n- Vertex $ 1 $ and vertex $ N_1 + N_2 $ are **initially disconnected**.\n\nWe are to **add exactly one edge** between a vertex $ u $ in $ [1, N_1] $ and a vertex $ v $ in $ [N_1+1, N_1+N_2] $. After adding this edge, the whole graph becomes connected (since both original components are internally connected), so there is **a path between 1 and $ N_1+N_2 $**.\n\nLet $ d $ be the **shortest path length** between vertex $ 1 $ and vertex $ N_1 + N_2 $ after adding the edge. We want to **maximize $ d $** by choosing the best possible edge $ (u,v) $ — that is, we are to compute the **maximum possible shortest path** between 1 and $ N_1+N_2 $ **over all valid choices of the connecting edge**.\n\n---\n\n### Strategy\n\nWe are to:\n- Compute distances from vertex 1 to all vertices in $ 1..N_1 $ (within the first component).\n- Compute distances from vertex $ N_1+N_2 $ to all vertices in $ N_1+1 .. N_1+N_2 $ (within the second component).\n- Then, for all possible connections $ (u,v) $ where $ u \\in [1,N_1] $, $ v \\in [N_1+1, N_1+N_2] $, the shortest path from 1 to $ N_1+N_2 $ becomes:\n  $$\n  d = \\text{dist1}[u] + 1 + \\text{dist2}[v]\n  $$\n  where:\n  - `dist1[u]`: shortest distance from vertex 1 to $ u $\n  - `dist2[v]`: shortest distance from vertex $ N_1+N_2 $ to $ v $\n\nWe can't try all $ N_1 \\times N_2 $ pairs (too large), so we do:\n\n- Precompute `dist1` for all $ u \\in [1, N_1] $\n- Precompute `dist2` for all $ v \\in [N_1+1, N_1+N_2] $\n- Then, define:\n  $$\n  A = \\max_{u=1}^{N_1} \\text{dist1}[u]\n  $$\n  $$\n  B = \\max_{v=N_1+1}^{N_1+N_2} \\text{dist2}[v]\n  $$\n  But **this is not sufficient** — we want to **maximize $ \\text{dist1}[u] + \\text{dist2}[v] + 1 $**.\n\nSo, the maximum $ d $ is:\n$$\n\\max_{u \\in [1,N_1], v \\in [N_1+1, N_1+N_2]} (\\text{dist1}[u] + \\text{dist2}[v] + 1)\n$$\n\nBut wait — is every pair (u,v) allowed? **No**, only those vertices $ u $ and $ v $ that actually **exist in the graph**. But since any pair can be connected (we can add any edge between the two components), every $ u \\in [1,N_1] $ and $ v \\in [N_1+1, N_1+N_2] $ is **a valid candidate**.\n\nSo, to **maximize $ d $**, we just need:\n$$\nd_{\\text{max}} = \\left( \\max_{u=1}^{N_1} \\text{dist1}[u] \\right) + \\left( \\max_{v=N_1+1}^{N_1+N_2} \\text{dist2}[v] \\right) + 1\n$$\n\nWait — is this correct?\n\n### No — **this is not correct**.\n\nWe can only connect **one pair (u,v)**. But we are **not required to connect the farthest nodes** — instead, we are to **choose (u,v)** such that the **resulting shortest path** from 1 to $ N_1+N_2 $ is **as long as possible**.\n\nBut here’s the catch: **after** adding edge (u,v), the path:\n$$\n1 \\to \\cdots \\to u \\to v \\to \\cdots \\to (N_1+N_2)\n$$\nhas length $ \\text{dist1}[u] + 1 + \\text{dist2}[v] $\n\nBut there might be **a shorter path** via some other added edge? No — we **only add one edge (u,v)**. So **only one connection** between components.\n\nSo **the only path from 1 to $ N_1+N_2 $** must go through $ u $ and $ v $. But wait — no! Once we fix $ (u,v) $, there is **only one bridge**, so **all** traffic between components goes via that edge.\n\nTherefore, the shortest path from 1 to $ N_1+N_2 $ **must use that bridge**, so the shortest path length is:\n$$\n\\min_{\\text{over all pairs (x,y) but only (u,v) exists}} \\text{dist1}[x] + 1 + \\text{dist2}[y]\n$$\nBut since only **(u,v)** is added, the path from 1 to $ N_1+N_2 $ must go from 1 → u → v → $ N_1+N_2 $, so:\n$$\nd = \\text{dist1}[u] + 1 + \\text{dist2}[v]\n$$\n\nWait — but **could there be a shorter path inside the components**? For example, maybe 1 → some other node in component 1 → u → v → some other node → $ N_1+N_2 $? But since we only added **one edge (u,v)**, the only possible transition is through $ u $ and $ v $, so yes — the path **must** go from 1 to $ u $, then cross, then $ v $ to $ N_1+N_2 $.\n\nBut hold on: the **shortest path** would take the **shortest route from 1 to u**, and from **v to $ N_1+N_2 $**.\n\nBut here’s the key insight: **we choose (u,v)** — so we are fixing one edge.\n\nFor a fixed choice of edge (u,v):\n\n$$\nd = \\text{dist1}[u] + 1 + \\text{dist2}[v]\n$$\n\nBut wait — is this necessarily the shortest path? Could there be **other paths** using (u,v) from 1 to $ N_1+N_2 $? No — because the only way to cross is via (u,v). So the shortest path from 1 to $ N_1+N_2 $ is:\n$$\n\\min_{\\text{only one bridge}} \\text{dist1}[x] + 1 + \\text{dist2}[y]\n$$\nBut $ x = u $, $ y = v $ — fixed! So only **one bridge**, so the only way is:\n- 1 → u (in first component): cost = dist1[u]\n- u → v: cost = 1\n- v → $ N_1+N_2 $: cost = dist2[v]\n\nSo yes, total distance = dist1[u] + 1 + dist2[v]\n\nThus, we can **choose u and v to maximize this quantity**, but we are constrained by the initial graph: dist1[u] and dist2[v] are fixed from the graph (we can’t change them), so we just compute:\n\n$$\n\\text{ans} = \\max_{u=1}^{N_1} \\text{dist1}[u] + \\max_{v=N_1+1}^{N_1+N_2} \\text{dist2}[v] + 1\n$$\n\nWait — again, is that valid?\n\nNo! Because **dist1[u] and dist2[v] are independent** — so **yes**, we can **maximize separately**.\n\nBecause we are free to choose **any u in [1,N_1]** and **any v in [N_1+1, N_1+N_2]** (there's no restriction on which pair to connect), the maximum of dist1[u] + dist2[v] + 1 is:\n\n$$\n(\\max \\text{dist1 in comp1}) + (\\max \\text{dist2 in comp2}) + 1\n$$\n\nBut wait — **no!** Because dist2 is from vertex $ N_1+N_2 $, so for a vertex $ v $ in the second component, dist2[v] = shortest distance from $ v $ to $ N_1+N_2 $. So we want vertex $ u $ **far from 1** and vertex $ v $ **far from $ N_1+N_2 $**? Wait —",
