    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a string s representing a 12-hour format time where some of the digits (possibly none) are replaced with a \"?\".\n12-hour times are formatted as \"HH:MM\", where HH is between 00 and 11, and MM is between 00 and 59. The earliest 12-hour time is 00:00, and the latest is 11:59.\nYou have to replace all the \"?\" characters in s with digits such that the time we obtain by the resulting string is a valid 12-hour format time and is the latest possible.\nReturn the resulting string.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: s = \"1?:?4\"\nOutput: \"11:54\"\nExplanation: The latest 12-hour format time we can achieve by replacing \"?\" characters is \"11:54\".\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: s = \"0?:5?\"\nOutput: \"09:59\"\nExplanation: The latest 12-hour format time we can achieve by replacing \"?\" characters is \"09:59\".\n\n \nConstraints:\n\ns.length == 5\ns[2] is equal to the character \":\".\nAll characters except s[2] are digits or \"?\" characters.\nThe input is generated such that there is at least one time between \"00:00\" and \"11:59\" that you can obtain after replacing the \"?\" characters.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solution(s):\n    \"\"\"\n    Replace all \"?\" characters in a 12-hour format time string to get the latest possible valid time.\n    \n    Args:\n        s (str): A string representing a 12-hour format time with some digits replaced by \"?\"\n                Format is \"HH:MM\" where HH is between 00-11 and MM is between 00-59\n    \n    Returns:\n        str: The latest possible valid 12-hour format time with all ? replaced by digits\n    \"\"\"\n    time_list = list(s)\n    \n    # Handle hours (positions 0 and 1)\n    # For the latest time, we want to maximize the hour value within 00-11 range\n    \n    # If first digit is \"?\" \n    if time_list[0] == \"?\":\n        # If second digit is \"0\", \"1\", \"2\", \"3\", or \"?\", we can use \"1\" for maximum hour\n        # If second digit is \"0\"-\"3\" or ?, the combination 1X where X is 0-3 is valid (10, 11, 12, 13)\n        # But in 12-hour format, max hour is 11, so we need to be careful\n        if time_list[1] == \"?\" or time_list[1] in \"01\":\n            time_list[0] = \"1\"\n        else:\n            # If second digit is 2-9, then first digit can only be 0 to stay within 00-11\n            time_list[0] = \"0\"\n    \n    # If second digit is \"?\"\n    if time_list[1] == \"?\":\n        # If first digit is \"1\", we can only go up to \"1\" (for 11, since 12+ is not valid in this context)\n        # If first digit is \"0\", we can go up to \"9\" (for 09)\n        if time_list[0] == \"1\":\n            time_list[1] = \"1\"\n        else:\n            time_list[1] = \"9\"\n    \n    # Handle minutes (positions 3 and 4)\n    # For the latest time, we want maximum minutes (59)\n    \n    # If third digit (first minute digit) is \"?\"\n    if time_list[3] == \"?\":\n        time_list[3] = \"5\"\n    \n    # If fourth digit (second minute digit) is \"?\"\n    if time_list[4] == \"?\":\n        time_list[4] = \"9\"\n    \n    return \"\".join(time_list)\n```",
