      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve():\n    t = int(input())\n    \n    for _ in range(t):\n        n, k = map(int, input().split())\n        s = input().strip()\n        \n        # We need to remove exactly k characters, so we'll have n-k characters left\n        remaining_length = n - k\n        \n        # Count frequency of each character\n        freq = {}\n        for char in s:\n            freq[char] = freq.get(char, 0) + 1\n        \n        # For a palindrome, at most one character can have odd frequency\n        # Count how many characters have odd frequency\n        odd_count = 0\n        for count in freq.values():\n            if count % 2 == 1:\n                odd_count += 1\n        \n        # We need to check if we can achieve a valid palindrome configuration\n        # with exactly n-k characters remaining\n        \n        # The minimum number of characters we need to remove to make the remaining\n        # string formable as a palindrome is related to the number of odd-frequency characters\n        # In a palindrome, at most one character can have odd count\n        \n        # The key insight: we can reduce odd_count by removing one character from an odd-frequency group\n        # Each removal from an odd-frequency character makes it even\n        # Each removal from an even-frequency character makes it odd\n        \n        # However, we can think differently: after removing k characters,\n        # what's the minimum odd_count we can achieve?\n        \n        # We can simulate: we want to minimize odd_count in the remaining string\n        # Strategy: try to make as many character frequencies even as possible\n        \n        # Let's think about parity changes:\n        # If we remove a character that appears odd times, it becomes even (reduces odd_count by 1)\n        # If we remove a character that appears even times, it becomes odd (increases odd_count by 1)\n        \n        # To minimize odd_count in the end, we should prefer removing from odd-frequency characters\n        # But we're constrained by the available characters\n        \n        # Alternate approach: \n        # Let's say after removal, we have some frequency distribution\n        # The key constraint is that in the remaining string, at most one character can have odd frequency\n        \n        # We can solve it by checking if it's possible to have at most 1 odd frequency\n        # in a string of length (n-k)\n        \n        # We can use a greedy approach:\n        # We want to minimize the number of odd frequencies in the remaining string\n        # The best we can do is to have 0 or 1 odd frequencies\n        \n        # Let's calculate the minimum possible odd_count we can achieve after removing exactly k characters\n        \n        # Start with current odd_count\n        # We can reduce odd_count by removing 1 from an odd-frequency character (odd -> even)\n        # We can increase odd_count by removing 1 from an even-frequency character (even -> odd)\n        \n        # Our moves: we have k removals\n        # We want to minimize final odd_count\n        # Best strategy: use as many removals as possible on odd-frequency characters\n        \n        available_odd_removals = 0\n        for char, count in freq.items():\n            if count % 2 == 1:\n                available_odd_removals += 1\n        \n        # We can also consider that we might need to remove more than just targeting odd frequency chars\n        # Let's simulate: we have k removals\n        # We can reduce odd_count by targeting odd-frequency chars\n        # But we can only reduce by at most min(k, available_odd_removals)\n        \n        # However, if we remove from even-frequency chars, we create new odd frequencies\n        remaining_removals = k\n        current_odd = odd_count\n        \n        # Best case: use removals on odd-frequency chars first (reduces odd count)\n        use_on_odd = min(remaining_removals, available_odd_removals)\n        current_odd -= use_on_odd\n        remaining_removals -= use_on_odd\n        \n        # Then use remaining removals on even-frequency chars (increases odd count)\n        current_odd += remaining_removals\n        \n        # Now current_odd is the minimum odd count we can achieve\n        # For a palindrome, we need current_odd <= 1\n        # Also need to check if remaining_length >= current_odd (which is always true if current_odd <= 1 and remaining_length >= 0)\n        \n        if current_odd <= 1 and remaining_length >= 0:\n            print(\"YES\")\n        else:\n            print(\"NO\")\n\nsolve()\n```",
