    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_211",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nYou are given an undirected graph G with N vertices and M edges.\r\nFor i = 1, 2, \\ldots, M, the i-th edge is an undirected edge connecting vertices u_i and v_i.\nA graph with N vertices is called good if the following condition holds for all i = 1, 2, \\ldots, K:\n\n- there is no path connecting vertices x_i and y_i in G.\n\nThe given graph G is good.\nYou are given Q independent questions. Answer all of them.\r\nFor i = 1, 2, \\ldots, Q, the i-th question is as follows.\n\n- Is the graph G^{{(i)}} obtained by adding an undirected edge connecting vertices p_i and q_i to the given graph G good?\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN M\r\nu_1 v_1\r\nu_2 v_2\r\n\\vdots\r\nu_M v_M\r\nK\r\nx_1 y_1\r\nx_2 y_2\r\n\\vdots\r\nx_K y_K\r\nQ\r\np_1 q_1\r\np_2 q_2\r\n\\vdots\r\np_Q q_Q\n\nOutput\n\nPrint Q lines.\r\nFor i = 1, 2, \\ldots, Q, the i-th line should contain the answer to the i-th question: Yes if the graph G^{{(i)}} is good, and No otherwise.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 2 \\leq N \\leq 2 \\times 10^5\n- 0 \\leq M \\leq 2 \\times10^5\n- 1 \\leq u_i, v_i \\leq N\n- 1 \\leq K \\leq 2 \\times 10^5\n- 1 \\leq x_i, y_i \\leq N\n- x_i \\neq y_i\n- i \\neq j \\implies \\lbrace x_i, y_i \\rbrace \\neq \\lbrace x_j, y_j \\rbrace\n- For all i = 1, 2, \\ldots, K, there is no path connecting vertices x_i and y_i.\n- 1 \\leq Q \\leq 2 \\times 10^5\n- 1 \\leq p_i, q_i \\leq N\n- p_i \\neq q_i\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n6 6\r\n1 2\r\n2 3\r\n2 3\r\n3 1\r\n5 4\r\n5 5\r\n3\r\n1 5\r\n2 6\r\n4 3\r\n4\r\n2 5\r\n2 6\r\n5 6\r\n5 4\n\nSample Output 1\n\nNo\r\nNo\r\nYes\r\nYes\r\n\n\n- For the first question, the graph G^{{(1)}} is not good because it has a path 1 \\rightarrow 2 \\rightarrow 5 connecting vertices x_1 = 1 and y_1 = 5. Therefore, print No.\n- For the second question, the graph G^{{(2)}} is not good because it has a path 2 \\rightarrow 6 connecting vertices x_2 = 2 and y_2 = 6. Therefore, print No.\n- For the third question, the graph G^{{(3)}} is good. Therefore, print Yes.\n- For the fourth question, the graph G^{{(4)}} is good. Therefore, print Yes.\n\nAs seen in this sample input, note that the given graph G may have self-loops or multi-edges.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import defaultdict, deque\n\ndef solve():\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().split()\n    \n    it = iter(data)\n    N = int(next(it)); M = int(next(it))\n    \n    parent = list(range(N + 1))\n    \n    def find(x):\n        if parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = find(parent[x])\n        return parent[x]\n    \n    def union(x, y):\n        px, py = find(x), find(y)\n        if px != py:\n            parent[px] = py\n    \n    edges = []\n    for _ in range(M):\n        u = int(next(it)); v = int(next(it))\n        edges.append((u, v))\n        if u != v:  # self-loop doesn't connect different components\n            union(u, v)\n    \n    K = int(next(it))\n    forbidden = []\n    for _ in range(K):\n        x = int(next(it)); y = int(next_it)\n        forbidden.append((x, y))\n    \n    # For each forbidden pair (x, y), we know they are in different components in the original graph\n    # After adding an edge (p, q), we need to check if any forbidden (x_i, y_i) become connected\n    \n    Q = int(next(it))\n    queries = []\n    for _ in range(Q):\n        p = int(next(it)); q = int(next(it))\n        queries.append((p, q))\n    \n    # Precompute roots for all nodes\n    roots = [find(i) for i in range(N + 1)]\n    \n    # Group nodes by component root\n    components = defaultdict(set)\n    for i in range(1, N + 1):\n        components[find(i)].add(i)\n    \n    # For each forbidden pair, note their component roots\n    # The graph remains good if after adding (p, q), no forbidden x_i, y_i end up in same component\n    \n    # But note: adding edge (p, q) will union components of p and q\n    # So for each query, we simulate the union of p and q, and check if any forbidden pair becomes connected\n    \n    # However, doing this naively for each query with DSU rebuild would be slow\n    # Instead, we precompute all component unions and store forbidden constraints\n    \n    # We can use the idea: after adding (p, q), the new component = comp[find(p)] U comp[find(q)]\n    # Then the graph becomes bad if there is any forbidden (x,y) such that find(x) == find(p or q after union) and find(y) == find(p or q after union)\n    \n    # Actually, simpler: the graph is NOT good if the added edge causes a forbidden pair (x,y) to be in the same component.\n    # For each forbidden (x,y), if x and y are in different components initially -> fine\n    # But after adding (p,q): if p and q connect two components, then all nodes in comp_p and comp_q become connected.\n    # So if there exists any forbidden pair (x,y) such that x in comp(p), y in comp(q) or vice versa, then after edge addition they become connected -> bad\n    # But note: if x and y are already in same component, that's impossible by input condition \"For all i, no path between x_i and y_i\"\n    \n    # So for each query, we check: does edge (p,q) connect two components, say A and B (if p and q are in same component already, then adding edge does nothing regarding connectivity strictly)\n    # But multiple edge won't change connectivity either\n    # So: if p and q are already in same component, then adding the edge doesn't connect new components -> previously forbidden pairs are still not connected -> still good\n    # Otherwise, merging comp(p) and comp(q). Then we need to check if there exists any forbidden (x_i, y_i) such that one is in comp(p) and other in comp(q) (or vice versa)\n    \n    # But we cannot iterate over all K forbidden pairs per query (Q up to 200k, K up to 200k) -> worst-case 40e9\n    \n    # Alternative idea:\n    # Preprocess: assign each node a component ID (the root of its component)\n    comp_id = {}\n    comp_map = {}\n    idx = 0\n    for root in components:\n        comp_id[root] = idx\n        comp_map[idx] = root\n        idx += 1\n    \n    node_to_comp = [0] * (N + 1)\n    for i in range(1, N + 1):\n        node_to_comp[i] = comp_id[find(i)]\n    \n    # Now collect for each component, which forbidden points start/end in it?\n    # But we need to know for each pair of components (c1, c2), whether there is a forbidden edge between them\n    forbidden_between_comps = set()\n    \n    for x, y in forbidden:\n        cx = node_to_comp[x]\n        cy = node_to_comp[y]\n        if cx > cy:\n            cx, cy = cy, cx\n        forbidden_between_comps.add((cx, cy))\n    \n    # Process each query\n    results = []\n    for p, q in queries:\n        cp_orig = node_to_comp[p]\n        cq_orig = node_to_comp[q]\n        \n        if cp_orig == cq_orig:\n            # already in same component, adding edge doesn't change connectivity -> still good\n            results.append(\"Yes\")\n        else:\n            # they are in different components\n            # simulate merging: use canonical ordering\n            c1, c2 = min(cp_orig, cq_orig), max(cp_orig, cq_orig)\n            # Check if (c1, c2) is a forbidden connection\n            if (c1, c2) in forbidden_between_comps:\n                results.append(\"No\")\n            else:\n                results.append(\"Yes\")\n    \n    # Print each result on a separate line\n    for res in results:\n        print(res)\n\nsolve()\n```",
