      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve():\n    import sys\n    \n    # Read input\n    line = sys.stdin.readline().strip()\n    N, A, B = map(int, line.split())\n    \n    line = sys.stdin.readline().strip()\n    D = list(map(int, line.split()))\n    \n    # Total days in a week\n    week_length = A + B\n    \n    # We need to check if there exists a starting day (holiday) such that all plans fall on holidays\n    # Since the cycle repeats every (A+B) days, we only need to check the possible starting positions\n    # within one cycle, but we can be smarter about this.\n    \n    # For each plan day D_i, we want D_i days from now to land on days 1 to A (holidays)\n    # If today is day t (1-indexed in the cycle), then D_i days later is (t + D_i - 1) % week_length + 1\n    # For this to be a holiday: (t + D_i - 1) % week_length + 1 <= A\n    # which means: (t + D_i - 1) % week_length < A\n    # or: t % week_length should be in certain ranges\n    \n    # Instead of checking all possible t, let's think differently\n    # For each D_i, the valid starting days t are such that:\n    # (t + D_i) % week_length is in [1, A] (if we think 1-indexed) or\n    # (t + D_i - 1) % week_length is in [0, A-1]\n    \n    # So t should satisfy: (t - 1 + D_i) % week_length < A\n    # This means t-1 should be in [(A - D_i) % week_length, (A + B - D_i) % week_length) in modular arithmetic\n    \n    # Alternative approach: check if there's any day of week that could be \"today\"\n    # such that all D_i days from now land on holidays\n    possible = False\n    \n    # We only need to check the holidays as possible starting days\n    # But since cycles repeat, we can check the constraints more directly\n    # For each plan, determine the constraint on the starting day\n    \n    # Let's consider the constraints on (start_day - 1) modulo week_length\n    # For plan i: (start_day - 1 + D_i) % week_length should be in [0, A-1]\n    # So (start_day - 1) % week_length should be in [( - D_i) % week_length, (A - D_i - 1) % week_length]\n    \n    # Instead, let's check possible remainders of (start_day - 1)\n    valid_start_remainders = set(range(week_length))  # initially all possible\n    \n    for d in D:\n        required_remainders = set()\n        # (start_r + d) % week_length < A\n        # start_r < A - d % week_length (modularly)\n        for r in range(week_length):\n            if (r + d) % week_length < A:\n                required_remainders.add(r)\n        \n        valid_start_remainders &= required_remainders\n        if not valid_start_remainders:\n            break\n    \n    if valid_start_remainders:\n        print(\"Yes\")\n    else:\n        print(\"No\")\n\nsolve()\n```",
