      "generated_answer": "We are given a sequence of operations on an initially empty undirected graph. Each operation adds a complete subgraph (clique) on a subset of vertices with a given cost. After all operations, we must determine:\n\n1. Whether the graph is connected.\n2. If yes, compute the **total weight of the MST**.\n\nWe cannot naively generate all edges (could be O(N²) per operation), as M and K_i can be large. But note the constraint:\n\n> Sum of all K_i ≤ 400,000\n\nSo the **total number of vertex appearances** is ≤ 400,000. However, within one operation, we would add edges between all pairs in a set of size K_i → this would be O(K_i²) edges. But we **cannot** add all edges explicitly, because that could be up to O(N²) per operation and too slow.\n\nBut here’s a key insight:\n\n> In a given operation, we are adding **complete edges** between all pairs in a subset S_i with **same weight** C_i.\n\nSo instead of adding all O(K_i²) edges, we notice:\n\n### Optimization:\nWe can simulate this by using **Kruskal's algorithm**, but only considering edges **from operations** – and crucially, for each operation, **we do not need to generate all edges** if we are smart.\n\nHowever, generating all O(K_i²) edges is too expensive (e.g., K_i = 1000 → 500,000 edges per op, and M up to 200000, worst case way too much).\n\nSo we must avoid generating every edge.\n\n---\n\n### Alternate Insight:\nWe can use the following trick:\n\nFor **each operation i**, instead of adding all O(K_i²) edges, we can **add only the necessary edges** to consider for MST.\n\nBut here’s the catch: **Since all edges in the operation have the same weight C_i**, we can simulate the effect of the operation by connecting the vertices **in a chain (path)** or **a star** with edges of weight C_i — but this does *not* simulate the complete graph!\n\nInstead, we can use this idea:\n\n> For MST purposes, if we have a set S_i and we want to have the option of connecting any pair with cost C_i, then **to emulate this operation**, we can add a **virtual (auxiliary) node** and connect all nodes in S_i to this node with weight C_i / 2 — but that's not suitable since C_i may be odd and introduces bias.\n\nAlternatively, much better:\n\n> **Instead of adding all O(K_i²) edges, just add a minimum spanning tree of the clique S_i using weight C_i**, i.e., connect the vertices in S_i with a **star or path** using **K_i - 1 edges of weight C_i**. This suffices because:\n\n- Any MST will only use an edge of weight C_i at most once per connected component — and we care about **minimum**.\n- But more importantly: **Adding all the edges from the complete graph is equivalent to making the entire set connected with edges of weight C_i**.\n\nSo if we instead connect the vertices in S_i with a **spanning tree of the subset** using **K_i - 1 edges of weight C_i**, we get the **same connectivity effect** (i.e., the subset becomes connected) and we preserve all relevant MST edge options.\n\nBut wait: could the MST pick a **smaller subset** of edges from a higher-index operation?\n\nYes — so we want to consider **edges** that could be used in MST. But since we are only allowed to use edges from the operations, and all such edges have weight C_i, the minimum edge weight available between any two nodes in S_i is at most C_i.\n\nSo, to avoid generating O(K_i²) edges, we can generate **only K_i - 1 edges** for each operation that **connect the subset S_i into a chain**:\n\ne.g., connect A[i][0] - A[i][1], A[i][1] - A[i][2], ..., A[i][K_i-2] - A[i][K_i-1], each with weight C_i.\n\nThis ensures the entire subset is connected **within this operation** using the given weight.\n\nBut is this sufficient for MST?\n\n**Yes**, because:\n\n- When running Kruskal, we **sort edges by weight**. So lower C_i are better.\n- If multiple operations can connect the same components, the one with smaller C_i will be picked first.\n- By adding a spanning tree (not all edges) of the subset, we ensure that we **preserve the possibility** of using C_i-weight edges to connect any components inside S_i. The MST will pick the cheapest ways to connect components, and having a spanning tree of S_i with weight C_i allows the algorithm to connect the nodes in any order.\n\nThis method is used in various graph compression tricks.\n\n---\n\n### Strategy:\n\n1. We process each operation.\n2. For each operation i:\n   - Read K_i, C_i, and list A of K_i vertices.\n   - Add **K_i - 1 edges**: connect consecutive vertices in A: (A[0], A[1]), (A[1], A[2]), ..., (A[K_i-2], A[K_i-1]), each with weight C_i.\n3. Collect all such edges.\n4. Sort all edges by weight.\n5. Run **Union-Find (Disjoint Set Union)** to:\n   - Build MST using Kruskal’s algorithm.\n   - Count total weight.\n6. Check if the final number of connected components is 1.\n\nBut wait: is this **correct**?\n\nYes — because:\n- We are not required to output the MST, only its total weight.\n- Adding a path through the subset S_i with edges of weight C_i allows the MST algorithm to potentially use one or more of these edges to connect components — just as if all edges were present.\n- Since all edges in the original operation have the same weight C_i, any subset of edges forming a spanning tree of S_i suffices to model the connectivity offered by the operation.\n- Furthermore, if a smaller-weight connection already exists between two nodes in S_i, then we don't need the higher-weight one anyway.\n\nHowever, caution: **Could we miss a cheaper way to connect across the set?**\nNo — because we are **not removing edges**, we are **offering possible connections** of cost C_i. The path ensures that if **any connection** from this operation can help connect different components, the algorithm will pick the cheapest available (by sorting all edges by weight).\n\nThis technique is known as **virtual clique building via linear chain**.\n\n---\n\n### Steps:\n\n1. Read N, M.\n2. Initialize empty list `edges`.\n3. For each of M operations:\n   - Read K, C\n   - Read K vertices into list A\n   - For j in range(K - 1):\n     - Append edge (A[j], A[j+1], C)\n4. Sort `edges` by weight.\n5. Union-Find over N nodes.\n6. Initialize MST weight = 0, components = N.\n7. For each edge (u, v, w) in sorted order:\n   - If u and v are not connected, unite them, add w to MST weight, components -= 1.\n8. If components != 1 → print -1\n   Else → print MST weight.\n\nBut wait: is this sufficient? What about multiple edges between same pair of nodes? The algorithm will naturally pick the smallest due to sorting.\n\nYes — and we don’t need to worry about duplicates since Union-Find skips already connected pairs.\n\nAlso, note: the input says vertices are given in increasing order, so the list A is sorted.\n\n---\n\n### Let’s test on Sample 1:\n\n**Input:**\n\n```\n4 3\n3 3\n1 2 3\n2 2\n1 2\n3 4\n1 3 4\n```\n\nOperation 1: K=3, C=3, A=[1,2,3] → add edges: (1-2,3), (2-3,3)  \nOperation 2: K=2, C=2, A=[1,2] → add edge: (1-2,2)  \nOperation 3: K=3, C=4, A=[1,3,4] → add edges: (1-3,4), (3-4,4)\n\nAll edges:\n- (1,2) w=3\n- (2,3) w=3\n- (1,2) w=2  ← better\n- (1,3) w=4\n- (3,4) w=4\n\nSort by weight:\n- (1,2) w=2\n- (1,2) w=3\n- (2,3) w=3\n- (1,3) w=4\n- (3,4) w=4\n\nNow build MST with 4 nodes:\n\n1. Take (1,2) w=2 → components: 3\n2. Take (2,3) w=3 → connects 3 → components: 2\n3. Take (3,4) w=4 → connects 4 → components: 1 → done.\n\nTotal = 2 + 3 + 4 = 9 → matches.\n\nBut wait, edge (2,3) w=3 is from op1. But node 2 and 3 are already connected? No:\nAfter op1, we added (2,3), but at",
