      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef getSmallestString(s: str) -> str:\n    n = len(s)\n    \n    # Convert string to list for easier manipulation\n    result = list(s)\n    \n    # Find the first character that is not 'a'\n    # We want to start our operation as early as possible\n    # but we need to be careful with 'a' because it becomes 'z'\n    i = 0\n    while i < n and s[i] == 'a':\n        i += 1\n    \n    # If all characters are 'a', we must convert some substring\n    # Converting the entire string would make it all 'z', which is bad\n    # Instead, we want to convert just enough to get the lexicographically smallest\n    if i == n:\n        # All are 'a', so we convert only the last character to 'z'\n        result[n-1] = 'z'\n        return ''.join(result)\n    \n    # We found the first non-'a' character at position i\n    # We want to apply the operation starting from a good position\n    # The optimal strategy is to find a segment that doesn't contain 'a'\n    # and start from the earliest possible position\n    \n    start = i\n    # Extend as long as we don't hit 'a' (because 'a' becomes 'z' which is worse)\n    while start > 0 and s[start-1] != 'a':\n        start -= 1\n    \n    # Now apply the operation from start to the end of consecutive non-'a' characters\n    end = start\n    while end < n and s[end] != 'a':\n        end += 1\n    \n    # Apply the operation on substring from start to end-1\n    for j in range(start, end):\n        result[j] = chr(ord(result[j]) - 1)\n    \n    return ''.join(result)\n```",
