e minimum of a group = min(selected_elements_from_0_to_i-1, nums[i])\n- But since the array is sorted, nums[i] ≥ all previous, so min = min(selected_elements_from_0_to_i-1) if any are selected, otherwise nums[i]\n\nFor groups where nums[i] is maximum:\n- Group with just [nums[i]] contributes: nums[i]^2 * nums[i] = nums[i]^3\n- Groups with nums[i] and some subset of {nums[0],...,nums[i-1]}:\n  - If no elements from 0 to i-1 are selected: contribution = nums[i]^3 (already counted)\n  - If elements from 0 to i-1 are selected: the minimum is the minimum of selected elements\n\nLet S be the sum of minimums of all non-empty subsets of {nums[0],...,nums[i-1]}.\nThen total contribution when nums[i] is maximum = nums[i]^2 * (nums[i] + S)\n\nThere's a known result: for a sorted array, the sum of minimums of all non-empty subsets is sum(nums[j] * 2^(n-j-1)) for j from 0 to n-1.\n\nBut I can build this incrementally:\nLet prefixSum[i] = sum of minimums of all non-empty subsets of {nums[0],...,nums[i]}\nThen prefixSum[i] = nums[i] + 2 * prefixSum[i-1]\n\nWait, let me verify:\n- Subsets of {nums[0],...,nums[i]}:\n  - Subsets not containing nums[i]: contribute prefixSum[i-1]\n  - Subsets containing nums[i]: \n    - {nums[i]} contributes nums[i]\n    - Any subset containing nums[i] and other elements: minimum is min(other_elements) since nums[i] is largest\n    - For each non-empty subset of {nums[0],...,nums[i-1]}, adding nums[i] doesn't change the minimum\n    - So subsets containing nums[i] contribute nums[i] + prefixSum[i-1]\n- Total: prefixSum[i-1] + (nums[i] + prefixSum[i-1]) = nums[i] + 2 * prefixSum[i-1]\n\nActually, let me reconsider the minimums:\nWhen we add nums[i] to subsets of {nums[0],...,nums[i-1]}:\n- The empty subset + nums[i] gives {nums[i]}, minimum = nums[i]\n- Each non-empty subset S of {nums[0],...,nums[i-1]} + nums[i] has minimum = min(S), same as without nums[i]\n\nSo sum of minimums = (sum of min for subsets not containing nums[i]) + (sum of min for subsets containing nums[i])\n= prefixSum[i-1] + (nums[i] + prefixSum[i-1]) \n= nums[i] + 2 * prefixSum[i-1]\n\nYes, that's correct.\n\nNow for our problem:\nWhen nums[i] is the maximum in a group:\n- The group must contain nums[i] \n- Can contain any subset of {nums[0],...,nums[i-1]}\n- There are 2^i possible such groups (including empty subset of previous elements)\n- The minimum of the group is:\n  - If no previous elements: minimum = nums[i]\n  - If some previous elements: minimum = minimum of the selected previous elements\n\nSo sum of minimums for all groups where nums[i] is maximum = \nsum of minimums of all subsets of {nums[0],...,nums[i-1]} where we treat the empty subset as having minimum nums[i]\n\nLet T = sum of minimums of all subsets of {nums[0],...,nums[i-1]} (including empty subset which we'll handle separately)\nActually, let's define:\n- Let S = sum of minimums of all non-empty subsets of {nums[0],...,nums[i-1]}\n- Then sum of minimums for our groups = S + nums[i] (for the case where no previous elements are selected)\n\nBut wait, \"all subsets\" includes empty. If we consider empty subset to contribute nums[i], and each non-empty subset contributes its minimum, then total = nums[i] + S.\n\nAnd we have the recurrence: S_i = nums[i] + 2*S_{i-1}, where S_i is sum of minimums of non-empty subsets of first i+1 elements.\n\nActually, let me define prefixMinSum[i] = sum of minimums of all non-empty subsets of {nums[0],...,nums[i]}.\nThen prefixMinSum[i] = nums[i] + 2 * prefixMinSum[i-1].\n\nBut for our purpose, when nums[i] is maximum:\n- sum of minimums of all valid groups = nums[i] + prefixMinSum[i-1]  (if i > 0, else just nums[i])\n\nTherefore, contribution = nums[i]^2 * (nums[i] + (prefixMinSum[i-1] if i > 0 else 0))\n\nWait, let me verify with the example:\nnums = [2,1,4] -> sorted: [1,2,4]\n\ni=0: nums[0]=1\n- groups: [1]\n- sum of minimums = 1\n- contribution = 1^2 * 1 = 1\n- Using formula: 1^2 * (1 + 0) = 1 ✓\n\ni=1: nums[1]=2  \n- prefixMinSum[0] = 1 (sum of min of non-empty subsets of [1])\n- sum of minimums = 2 + 1 = 3\n- groups: [2] (min=2), [2,1] (min=1) -> sum of mins = 2+1=3 ✓\n- contribution = 2^2 * 3 = 12\n\ni=2: nums[2]=4\n- prefixMinSum[1] = sum of min of non-empty subsets of [1,2]\n- subsets: [1]→1, [2]→2, [1,2]→1, sum = 1+2+1=4\n- recurrence: prefixMinSum[1] = 2 + 2*prefixMinSum[0] = 2 + 2*1 = 4 ✓\n- sum of minimums = 4 + 4 = 8\n- groups: [4]→4, [4,1]→1, [4,2]→2, [4,1,2]→1, sum = 4+1+2+1=8 ✓\n- contribution = 4^2 * 8 = 128\n\nTotal = 1 + 12 + 128 = 141 ✓\n\nPerfect!\n\n```python\ndef sumOfPower(nums):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    nums.sort()\n    n = len(nums)\n    \n    total_power = 0\n    prefix_min_sum = 0  # sum of minimums of all non-empty subsets of previous elements\n    \n    for i in range(n):\n        # When nums[i] is the maximum, sum of minimums of all valid groups\n        sum_of_minimums = (nums[i] + prefix_min_sum) % MOD\n        \n        # Contribution to total power\n        max_squared = (nums[i] * nums[i]) % MOD\n        contribution = (max_squared * sum_of_minimums) %",
