    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_137",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are at a fruit market with different types of exotic fruits on display.\nYou are given a 1-indexed array prices, where prices[i] denotes the number of coins needed to purchase the i^th fruit.\nThe fruit market has the following offer:\n\nIf you purchase the i^th fruit at prices[i] coins, you can get the next i fruits for free.\n\nNote that even if you can take fruit j for free, you can still purchase it for prices[j] coins to receive a new offer.\nReturn the minimum number of coins needed to acquire all the fruits.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: prices = [3,1,2]\nOutput: 4\nExplanation: You can acquire the fruits as follows:\n- Purchase the 1^st fruit with 3 coins, you are allowed to take the 2^nd fruit for free.\n- Purchase the 2^nd fruit with 1 coin, you are allowed to take the 3^rd fruit for free.\n- Take the 3^rd fruit for free.\nNote that even though you were allowed to take the 2^nd fruit for free, you purchased it because it is more optimal.\nIt can be proven that 4 is the minimum number of coins needed to acquire all the fruits.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: prices = [1,10,1,1]\nOutput: 2\nExplanation: You can acquire the fruits as follows:\n- Purchase the 1^st fruit with 1 coin, you are allowed to take the 2^nd fruit for free.\n- Take the 2^nd fruit for free.\n- Purchase the 3^rd fruit for 1 coin, you are allowed to take the 4^th fruit for free.\n- Take the 4^t^h fruit for free.\nIt can be proven that 2 is the minimum number of coins needed to acquire all the fruits.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= prices.length <= 1000\n1 <= prices[i] <= 10^5",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to find the minimum coins to acquire all fruits with the offer: when you purchase fruit i, you get the next i fruits for free.\n\nKey insights:\n1. I can choose to purchase any fruit (paying its price) or take it for free if covered by a previous purchase\n2. Even if a fruit is available for free, I might want to purchase it to get a new offer\n3. This is a dynamic programming problem where I need to track what fruits I've already covered\n\nLet me trace through the examples:\n- Example 1: prices = [3,1,2]\n  - If I buy fruit 1 (cost 3), I get fruits 2 for free (cover 1 more)\n  - But if I also buy fruit 2 (cost 1), I get fruit 3 for free (cover 2 more)\n  - Total cost: 3 + 1 = 4\n\n- Example 2: prices = [1,10,1,1]\n  - Buy fruit 1 (cost 1), get fruit 2 for free (cover 1 more)\n  - Buy fruit 3 (cost 1), get fruit 4 for free (cover 3 more)  \n  - Total cost: 1 + 1 = 2\n\nI'll use dynamic programming where `dp[i]` represents the minimum cost to acquire all fruits from position i onwards, given that fruits 1 to i-1 are already handled.\n\n```python\ndef minimumCoins(prices):\n    n = len(prices)\n    # dp[i] = minimum cost to acquire fruits from position i to n\n    dp = [float('inf')] * (n + 2)\n    dp[n + 1] = 0  # Base case: no more fruits to acquire\n    \n    # Fill dp table from right to left\n    for i in range(n, 0, -1):\n        # Option 1: Purchase fruit i, which allows us to get next i fruits for free\n        # This means we only need to pay for fruit i, and we can skip paying for fruits i+1 to i+i\n        # But we still need to solve for fruits beyond i+i\n        next_uncovered = min(i + i + 1, n + 1)  # First fruit not covered by this purchase\n        dp[i] = prices[i-1] + dp[next_uncovered]\n        \n        # Option 2: If fruit i can be taken for free by a previous purchase,\n        # we don't need to pay for it, but this is handled by the DP recurrence\n        # The key is that we always consider purchasing at each position,\n        # and the free option is implicit when a previous purchase covers this fruit\n        \n        # Actually, we need to consider that we might not need to purchase at i\n        # if it's already covered, but the DP will naturally handle the optimal choice\n        # through the recurrence.\n        \n        # The above assignment already captures the essence - at each position i,\n        # we must either have it covered by a previous purchase (in which case\n        # the cost is already accounted for) or we purchase it here.\n        \n        # Since we're going backwards, we need to ensure we're not double-counting\n        # Actually, let me reconsider the approach.\n        \n    # Let me use a different approach: dp[i] = min cost to acquire fruits 1 to i\n    # But that might not work well with the offer structure.\n    \n    # Better approach: let's use dp[i] where i is the current position we're considering\n    # and we track the furthest fruit that's covered by previous purchases.\n    \n    # Actually, let me use a simpler DP: from each position, try purchasing and see the optimal\n    memo = {}\n    \n    def dfs(pos, covered_upto):\n        # pos: current fruit position we're considering (1-indexed)\n        # covered_upto: the furthest position that is already covered (can be taken for free)\n        \n        if pos > n:\n            return 0\n            \n        if (pos, covered_upto) in memo:\n            return memo[(pos, covered_upto)]\n        \n        # If current fruit is already covered, we can take it for free\n        if pos <= covered_upto:\n            result = dfs(pos + 1, covered_upto)\n        else:\n            # Fruit is not covered, we must purchase it\n            # Option: purchase fruit pos, which covers up to pos + pos\n            result = prices[pos-1] + dfs(pos + 1, max(covered_upto, pos + pos))\n            \n            # We could consider not purchasing here, but if it's not covered,\n            # we have no choice but to purchase (since we can't get it for free)\n            # So the above is the only option when pos > covered_upto\n            \n        memo[(pos, covered_upto)] = result\n        return result\n    \n    return dfs(1, 0)\n```",
      "success": true,
