      "generated_answer": "We are given `N` dice, where die `i` rolls an integer uniformly from `1` to `A_i`.  \nWe want the probability that **there exists a subset of the rolled dice whose sum is exactly 10**, when all dice are rolled.\n\nWe are to compute this probability modulo **998244353**, interpreted as the modular inverse of the denominator times the numerator.\n\n---\n\n### Strategy:\n\nWe will use **dynamic programming (DP)** over subsets and sums, but since dice are independent, we can use a **sum DP** that tracks the probability of achieving each possible sum via some subset.\n\nHowever, note that:\n\n- `N ≤ 100`, but the sum we care about is just `10`, which is very small.\n- `A_i` can be up to 1e6, but the values that exceed 10 don't need full handling beyond their effect on probabilities.\n\nWe aim to compute the **probability that *some* subset of the dice (after rolling) adds to 10**.\n\nWe can use **inclusion-exclusion** or **DP over dice and achievable subset sums up to 10**.\n\nLet’s use **DP with generating functions** over subsets.\n\nIdea:\n\nLet `dp[s]` be the probability that after processing some dice, the subset sums we can form include certain states — but we need to track possible sets of subset sums? That’s exponential.\n\nInstead, for small target sum (10), we can use **subset-sum style DP** with probabilities.\n\nBut the key complexity: dice are rolled randomly; for each die, we are uncertain about the value it contributes.\n\nWe need to compute the probability that **some non-empty (any) subset** of the dice (post-roll) sums to 10.\n\nWe can use **DP over dice**, and for each die, update the distribution over possible subset sums that we can achieve, considering we can either include or exclude the die.\n\nBut note: **each die contributes only one fixed value after roll**, but we don’t know it. So we must average over all possible rolls.\n\nWe can use **expectation-style convolution**, tracking for each possible subset sum (from 0 to 10), the probability that this sum can be achieved by some subset.\n\nBUT: different rolls lead to different possible values, and inclusion of a die depends on its outcome.\n\nWe'll use **DP[s]** = probability that the subset sum `s` is achievable (for s from 0 to 10). But probabilities are not independent (achieving sum 5 and 6 may be correlated), so tracking only `dp[s]` is insufficient.\n\nInstead, we use a **state** representing the set of achievable subset sums (up to 10), but since max sum is 10, there are `2^11 = 2048` possible states (from 0 to 10). That's feasible!\n\nLet’s represent the state as a bitmask of length 11 (sums 0 to 10). A bit at position `s` means sum `s` is achievable.\n\nWe’ll do DP over dice, with:\n```python\ndp[mask] = probability that the set of achievable subset sums is exactly represented by `mask`\n```\nBut that's too heavy? But note: N ≤ 100, but number of masks is 2048, and each die we iterate over all masks and all possible roll values → 2048 × 100 × (max A_i) → but A_i can be up to 10^6 → too slow.\n\nBut we don't need to iterate over `A_i` directly.\n\nFor each die `i`, it rolls a value `v` in `[1, A_i]`, each with probability `1 / A_i`.\n\nBut note: for values `v > 10`, they can never be part of a subset summing to 10 unless combined with others, but if `v > 10`, it cannot be used **at all** in any subset sum ≤ 10.\n\nSo:\n- If `v > 10`, then this die can only be **not included** in any subset contributing to sum 10.\n- If `v ≤ 10`, then we may include it or not.\n\nBut more precisely: when the die shows `v`, we can choose to **include** it in a subset or not.\n\nSo for a given die with roll value `v`, the effect on the subset sums is:\n- New achievable sums = old achievable sums ∪ (old achievable sums + v)\n\nBut capped at 10.\n\nSo we can do DP over `mask` (subset sums 0–10), and update with each die.\n\nWe initialize with mask `1 << 0` (sum 0 is always achievable by selecting no die), with probability 1.\n\nFor each die `i`, we consider:\n- For each possible value `v` in `1..A_i`, which happens with probability `1 / A_i`\n  - If `v > 10`: then including this die in any subset that contributes to sum ≤ 10 is impossible (since v ≥ 11), so we cannot use it. So the set of achievable subset sums **does not change**.\n  - If `v ≤ 10`: then we can either include or not include. The new set of achievable sums is the union of:\n    - old sums\n    - old sums shifted by `v`, capped at 10\n\nSo the update: new_mask = old_mask OR (old_mask << v), with bits above 10 masked out.\n\nSince `v ≤ 10` and max sum we care about is 10, we can do this.\n\nBut we have to average over all `v`.\n\nSo for each die:\n- Let `total_prob_contribution = 0`\n- Count how many values `v` in `[1, A_i]` are > 10 → they contribute identity update.\n- For `v <= 10`, we branch.\n\nBut: A_i can be large (up to 1e6), but number of distinct `v` we care about (<=10) is at most 10: `1 to min(A_i,10)`\n\nSo we can do:\n\n```python\nFor die i:\n    for each mask in current state with probability dp[mask]:\n        let new_dp[mask] += (number of v > 10) / A_i * dp[mask]\n        for v from 1 to min(A_i, 10):\n            new_mask = mask | ((mask << v) & full_mask)   # full_mask = (1<<11)-1\n            new_dp[new_mask] += 1/A_i * dp[mask]\n```\n\nBut we are working modulo 998244353 and with fractions.\n\nInstead, we work with **integers mod 998244353**, and represent probabilities as **modular inverses**.\n\nLet’s maintain `dp[mask]` as a fraction mod 998244353.\n\nTotal number of outcomes: `total = ∏ A_i`\n\nWe can simulate die-by-die, and at each step multiply by `1/A_i` → so we accumulate probability as we go.\n\nSo algorithm:\n\n- Let `dp` be a dictionary (or array) of size 2048: `dp[mask]` = probability (mod 998244353) that current achievable subset sums are `mask`\n- Initialize: `dp[1 << 0] = 1` (only sum 0 is achievable)\n- full_mask = (1 << 11) - 1  # bits 0..10\n- For each die with max value `A`:\n    - new_dp = new array of zeros\n    - Let `invA = pow(A, -1, 998244353)`\n    - Let `large_v_count = max(0, A - 10)`  # values v > 10: they don't extend sum set\n    - For each mask in [0, 2047]:\n        - prob = dp[mask]\n        - If prob is 0, skip\n        - Case 1: v > 10 → contributes `large_v_count * invA * prob` to `new_dp[mask]`\n        - Case 2: v in [1, min(A,10)]:\n            - for each such `v`:\n                - new_mask = mask | ((mask << v) & full_mask)\n                - then: new_dp[new_mask] += invA * prob (mod mod)\n    - Set dp = new_dp\n\n- After all dice, the answer is the sum of `dp[mask]` over all masks where bit 10 is set.\n\nBut note: the mask includes sum 0 initially, and we build up.\n\nLet's test with sample:\n```\nInput: \n4\n1 7 2 9\n```\n\nDie 1: A=1 → only value v=1\n- mask starts: 1<<0 = 1 (binary: ...00000000001)\n- v=1: new_mask = 1 | (1<<1) = 1 | 2 = 3 → sums 0 and 1\n- so after die1: mask=3, prob=1\n\nDie2: A=7 → v in 1..7 (all <=10)\n- For each v in 1..7:\n  - each with prob 1/7",
