      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to find the team that will be the champion - the team that no other team is stronger than.\n\nLet me analyze the examples:\n- Example 1: grid = [[0,1],[0,0]]\n  - grid[0][1] = 1 means team 0 is stronger than team 1\n  - grid[1][0] = 0 means team 1 is NOT stronger than team 0 (so team 0 is stronger)\n  - So team 0 is stronger than team 1, making team 0 the champion\n\n- Example 2: grid = [[0,0,1],[1,0,1],[0,0,0]]\n  - Team 1 beats team 0 (grid[1][0] = 1)\n  - Team 1 beats team 2 (grid[1][2] = 1)\n  - Team 0 beats team 2 (grid[0][2] = 1)\n  - No team beats team 1, so team 1 is the champion\n\nThe key insight is that the champion team is the one that has beaten all other teams. In other words, for team i to be champion, grid[i][j] should be 1 for all j where j != i.\n\nLooking at this more carefully, I can see that:\n- If team a beats team b, then grid[a][b] = 1\n- The champion should have the most wins (highest row sum), but more importantly, the champion should have no losses.\n\nActually, let me re-read: \"Team a will be the champion of the tournament if there is no team b that is stronger than team a.\"\n\nThis means team a is champion if no other team beats team a. For any team b where b != a, we need grid[b][a] = 0 (meaning team b is NOT stronger than team a).\n\nSo I need to find the team where no other team has a 1 in the column corresponding to that team.\n\nWait, let me reconsider. The condition is that team i is stronger than team j if grid[i][j] == 1.\n\nSo team j is stronger than team i if grid[j][i] == 1.\n\nFor team a to be champion, there should be no team b such that team b is stronger than team a, which means there should be no team b where grid[b][a] == 1.\n\nSo I need to find the team a where column a has all zeros (except the diagonal which is always 0 by constraint).\n\nBut let me verify with the examples:\n- Example 1: column 0 has [0,0] - no ones, so team 0 is not beaten by anyone\n- Example 2: column 1 has [0,0,0] - no ones, so team 1 is not beaten by anyone\n\nAlternatively, I can think of it as finding the team that beats everyone else. In a tournament with clear dominance relationships, there should be exactly one team that beats all others.\n\nLooking at the constraints about transitivity, this suggests there's a clear ranking.\n\nFrom the examples, the champion seems to be the one with the most 1's in their row (most wins).\n\nLet me check:\n- Example 1: row 0 has sum 1, row 1 has sum 0, champion is 0\n- Example 2: row 0 has sum 2, row 1 has sum 2, row 2 has sum 0... wait, both row 0 and row 1 have sum 2.\n\nActually, looking more carefully at example 2:\n- grid = [[0,0,1],[1,0,1],[0,0,0]]\n- row 0: [0,0,1] -> sum = 1 (team 0 beats team 2)\n- row 1: [1,0,1] -> sum = 2 (team 1 beats team 0 and team 2) \n- row 2: [0,0,0] -> sum = 0\n\nSince team 1 beats team 0 and team 2, team 1 is champion. So it's the team with the maximum number of wins.\n\nBut is it possible to have ties? The constraint about transitivity suggests there's a total ordering, so there should be exactly one team that beats everyone it's compared against.\n\nActually, the simplest approach is: the champion is the team that has no team stronger than it. So for team i, check if there's any team j where grid[j][i] == 1. If not, team i is champion.\n\n```python\ndef findChampion(grid):\n    n = len(grid)\n    \n    for team in range(n):\n        is_champion = True\n        for other_team in range(n):\n            if other_team != team and grid[other_team][team] == 1:\n                is_champion = False\n                break\n        if is_champion:\n            return team\n    \n    return -1  # This should never happen given the constraints\n```",
