      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve():\n    S = input().strip()\n    T = input().strip()\n    \n    # The set of letters that @ can be replaced with\n    atcoder = set('atcoder')\n    \n    # Count the characters in each string, treating @ specially\n    count_S = {}\n    count_T = {}\n    \n    for c in S:\n        if c == '@':\n            # @ can become any of 'atcoder', so we'll handle it later\n            continue\n        count_S[c] = count_S.get(c, 0) + 1\n    \n    for c in T:\n        if c == '@':\n            # @ can become any of 'atcoder', so we'll handle it later\n            continue\n        count_T[c] = count_T.get(c, 0) + 1\n    \n    # Count @ symbols in each string\n    at_count_S = S.count('@')\n    at_count_T = T.count('@')\n    \n    # Check if we can make the two strings equal by rearranging and replacing @\n    # For each character that appears in either string\n    all_chars = set(S + T) - {'@'}\n    \n    # First check: all non-@, non-atcoder letters must match exactly\n    for char in all_chars:\n        if char != '@' and char not in atcoder:\n            s_count = count_S.get(char, 0)\n            t_count = count_T.get(char, 0)\n            if s_count != t_count:\n                print(\"No\")\n                return\n    \n    # For letters in 'atcoder', we can use @ to fill gaps\n    for char in atcoder:\n        s_count = count_S.get(char, 0)\n        t_count = count_T.get(char, 0)\n        \n        # If one string has more of this character than the other,\n        # we need to use @ cards to make up the difference\n        if s_count > t_count:\n            # S has more 'char' than T, so T needs (s_count - t_count) @ cards to become 'char'\n            additional_needed = s_count - t_count\n            at_count_T -= additional_needed\n        elif t_count > s_count:\n            # T has more 'char' than S, so S needs (t_count - s_count) @ cards to become 'char'\n            additional_needed = t_count - s_count\n            at_count_S -= additional_needed\n    \n    # Check if we used negative @ cards (which means we needed more than we had)\n    if at_count_S < 0 or at_count_T < 0:\n        print(\"No\")\n        return\n    \n    # Check remaining characters not in atcoder\n    for char in all_chars:\n        if char != '@' and char not in atcoder:\n            s_count = count_S.get(char, 0)\n            t_count = count_T.get(char, 0)\n            if s_count != t_count:\n                print(\"No\")\n                return\n    \n    # Any remaining @ cards can be turned into any letter in 'atcoder',\n    # but they must match each other. So we need the same number of remaining @ cards.\n    # Actually, since we can rearrange freely, we just need to ensure we didn't run out.\n    print(\"Yes\")\n\nsolve()\n```",
