    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a string s. s[i] is either a lowercase English letter or '?'.\nFor a string t having length m containing only lowercase English letters, we define the function cost(i) for an index i as the number of characters equal to t[i] that appeared before it, i.e. in the range [0, i - 1].\nThe value of t is the sum of cost(i) for all indices i.\nFor example, for the string t = \"aab\":\n\ncost(0) = 0\ncost(1) = 1\ncost(2) = 0\nHence, the value of \"aab\" is 0 + 1 + 0 = 1.\n\nYour task is to replace all occurrences of '?' in s with any lowercase English letter so that the value of s is minimized.\nReturn a string denoting the modified string with replaced occurrences of '?'. If there are multiple strings resulting in the minimum value, return the lexicographically smallest one.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput:   s = \"???\" \nOutput:   \"abc\" \nExplanation:  In this example, we can replace the occurrences of '?' to make s equal to \"abc\".\nFor \"abc\", cost(0) = 0, cost(1) = 0, and cost(2) = 0.\nThe value of \"abc\" is 0.\nSome other modifications of s that have a value of 0 are \"cba\", \"abz\", and, \"hey\".\nAmong all of them, we choose the lexicographically smallest.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput:  s = \"a?a?\"\nOutput:  \"abac\"\nExplanation:  In this example, the occurrences of '?' can be replaced to make s equal to \"abac\".\nFor \"abac\", cost(0) = 0, cost(1) = 0, cost(2) = 1, and cost(3) = 0.\nThe value of \"abac\" is 1.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= s.length <= 10^5\ns[i] is either a lowercase English letter or '?'.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef minimizeStringValue(s):\n    s = list(s)\n    n = len(s)\n    \n    # Count fixed characters and positions of '?'\n    fixed_count = {}\n    q_positions = []\n    \n    for i, char in enumerate(s):\n        if char == '?':\n            q_positions.append(i)\n        else:\n            fixed_count[char] = fixed_count.get(char, 0) + 1\n    \n    # Determine how many replacements we need\n    q_count = len(q_positions)\n    \n    if q_count == 0:\n        return ''.join(s)\n    \n    # Strategy: To minimize cost, we want to distribute replacement letters\n    # as evenly as possible among the 26 letters, prioritizing lexicographically\n    # smaller letters when there are ties.\n    \n    # Start with current counts (including fixed characters)\n    char_count = fixed_count.copy()\n    for c in 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz':\n        if c not in char_count:\n            char_count[c] = 0\n    \n    # Determine optimal letters for replacement\n    # For each replacement, pick the letter with minimum current count\n    # (and lexicographically smallest in case of ties)\n    replacement_letters = []\n    \n    for _ in range(q_count):\n        # Find character with minimum count (lexicographically smallest in case of tie)\n        best_char = 'a'\n        min_count = char_count['a']\n        \n        for c in 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz':\n            if char_count[c] < min_count:\n                min_count = char_count[c]\n                best_char = c\n        \n        replacement_letters.append(best_char)\n        char_count[best_char] += 1\n    \n    # Sort replacement letters to achieve lexicographically smallest result\n    # We need to be careful here - we want to assign smaller letters to earlier positions\n    # but also consider the impact on cost\n    replacement_letters.sort()\n    \n    # Assign replacement letters to '?' positions\n    for i, pos in enumerate(q_positions):\n        s[pos] = replacement_letters[i]\n    \n    return ''.join(s)\n```",
