      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import defaultdict, deque\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read input\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    n = int(data[0])\n    \n    # Build adjacency list\n    adj = defaultdict(list)\n    degree = [0] * (n + 1)\n    \n    edges = []\n    index = 1\n    for _ in range(n - 1):\n        u = int(data[index])\n        v = int(data[index + 1])\n        index += 2\n        adj[u].append(v)\n        adj[v].append(u)\n        degree[u] += 1\n        degree[v] += 1\n    \n    # If vertex 1 is already a leaf, we can remove it immediately\n    if degree[1] <= 1:\n        print(1)\n        return\n    \n    # We'll simulate the deletion process using a BFS-like approach from leaves\n    # But we need to keep vertex 1 until the end, so we consider the structure\n    \n    # We'll do a BFS starting from all leaves except vertex 1\n    # We want to remove everything that can be removed without affecting the path to preserve 1 until the end\n    \n    # Instead, think: We want to remove vertex 1. To do that, we must first make it a leaf.\n    # So we need to remove all but one of its neighbors.\n    \n    # The key insight: \n    # We want to remove vertex 1. It will become removable when it becomes a leaf (degree <= 1).\n    # So we need to remove enough of its neighbors so that only one remains (or zero if it's the last one).\n    \n    # We can model this as: \n    # The minimum number of operations is the size of a minimum connected subtree containing vertex 1 \n    # such that all other subtrees attached to vertex 1 can be pruned first.\n    \n    # Alternate approach:\n    # We do a DFS from vertex 1, and for each subtree attached to vertex 1, we compute how many deletions\n    # we can avoid if we keep that path alive until the end.\n    \n    # Actually, a simpler way:\n    # We want to compute how many nodes must be deleted before vertex 1 becomes a leaf.\n    # This includes:\n    #   - All nodes in the subtrees of vertex 1's children, except possibly one path to delay deletion\n    # But note: we can leave one branch intact (we don't have to delete all), so we can save one branch.\n    \n    # Important observation: We can delay deleting one entire branch from vertex 1, so that when we finally delete vertex 1,\n    # it only has one neighbor left.\n    #\n    # So answer = total number of nodes - the size of the largest subtree rooted at one of vertex 1's children\n    \n    # But wait: look at sample 1: vertex 1 has neighbors 2 and 6.\n    # Subtree of 2 has nodes: 2,3,4,5 -> size 4\n    # Subtree of 6 has nodes: 6,7,8,9 -> size 4\n    # Then answer would be 9 - 4 = 5 -> matches\n    #\n    # Sample 2: vertex 1 has neighbor 2. Subtree of 2 has nodes: 2,3,4,5,6 -> size 5\n    # answer = 6 - 5 = 1 -> matches\n    #\n    # So algorithm:\n    # 1. Start BFS/DFS from vertex 1, compute the sizes of the subtrees for each branch from vertex 1.\n    # 2. Answer = N - max(subtree_size) for each neighbor of vertex 1\n    \n    visited = [False] * (n + 1)\n    subtree_size = [0] * (n + 1)\n    \n    def dfs(u, parent):\n        size = 1\n        for v in adj[u]:\n            if v != parent:\n                size += dfs(v, u)\n        subtree_size[u] = size\n        return size\n    \n    dfs(1, -1)\n    \n    # Get the sizes of the subtrees of each neighbor of vertex 1\n    max_subtree = 0\n    for neighbor in adj[1]:\n        max_subtree = max(max_subtree, subtree_size[neighbor])\n    \n    answer = n - max_subtree\n    print(answer)\n\nsolve()\n```",
