      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef maximumStrength(nums, k):\n    n = len(nums)\n    # dp[i][j][0] = max strength using first i elements, selecting j subarrays, and not having a subarray ending at i-1\n    # dp[i][j][1] = max strength using first i elements, selecting j subarrays, and having a subarray ending at i-1\n    \n    # We'll use a 3D DP approach but optimize space by only keeping previous row\n    prev_dp = [[float('-inf'), float('-inf')] for _ in range(k + 1)]\n    prev_dp[0][0] = 0  # Base case: 0 subarrays, 0 strength\n    \n    for i in range(n):\n        curr_dp = [[float('-inf'), float('-inf')] for _ in range(k + 1)]\n        \n        for j in range(k + 1):\n            # Option 1: Don't use current element\n            curr_dp[j][0] = max(prev_dp[j][0], prev_dp[j][1])\n            \n            if j > 0:\n                # Calculate multiplier for jth subarray: (-1)^(j+1) * (k - j + 1)\n                multiplier = (1 if j % 2 == 1 else -1) * (k - j + 1)\n                element_contribution = nums[i] * multiplier\n                \n                # Option 2: Start new subarray at current element\n                curr_dp[j][1] = max(curr_dp[j][1], prev_dp[j-1][0] + element_contribution, prev_dp[j-1][1] + element_contribution)\n                \n                # Option 3: Extend existing subarray ending at previous element\n                if j > 0:\n                    curr_dp[j][1] = max(curr_dp[j][1], prev_dp[j][1] + element_contribution)\n        \n        prev_dp = curr_dp\n    \n    # Return maximum of ending with or without subarray at last position\n    return max(prev_dp[k][0], prev_dp[k][1])\n```",
