    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve(x, y, z):\n    # We have x strings of \"AA\", y strings of \"BB\", z strings of \"AB\"\n    # We want to maximize length without \"AAA\" or \"BBB\" as substrings\n    \n    # Strategy: We can't have 3 consecutive A's or B's\n    # \"AA\" contributes 2 A's, \"BB\" contributes 2 B's, \"AB\" contributes 1 A and 1 B\n    \n    # Key insights:\n    # 1. \"AB\" strings can be placed freely since they don't create AAA or BBB by themselves\n    # 2. We need to be careful with AA and BB placement to avoid triple letters\n    \n    # We can alternate AA and BB strings to prevent AAA and BBB\n    # The optimal pattern is to interleave AAs and BBs\n    \n    if x == y:\n        # Equal number of AA and BB - we can alternate perfectly\n        # Pattern: AA, BB, AA, BB, ...\n        # AA and BB can be fully used\n        return 2*x + 2*y + 2*z\n    elif x > y:\n        # More AA than BB\n        # Pattern: BB, AA, BB, AA, ... then remaining AA\n        # After alternating, we can add at most 1 more AA (otherwise AAA formed)\n        return 2*y + 2*y + 2*(x-y <= 1) + 2*z\n    else:\n        # More BB than AA\n        # Pattern: AA, BB, AA, BB, ... then remaining BB\n        # After alternating, we can add at most 1 more BB (otherwise BBB formed)\n        return 2*x + 2*x + 2*(y-x <= 1) + 2*z\n```\n\nWait, let me reconsider this more carefully:\n\n```python\ndef solve(x, y, z):\n    # The key is that we can always use all z \"AB\" strings since they don't create AAA or BBB\n    \n    # For AA and BB strings, we need to avoid triplets\n    # We can alternate AA and BB optimally\n    \n    # If we have min(x,y) pairs of alternating AA and BB, we get 4*min(x,y) characters\n    # After alternating, we can add at most one more AA or BB (but not both)\n    \n    if x == y:\n        # Perfect alternation possible - can use all AA and BB\n        return 2*x + 2*y + 2*z\n    else:\n        # Use min(x,y) pairs of alternation, then potentially 1 more of the excess\n        # The excess AA/BB can only be added if it doesn't create AAA/BBB\n        # When we alternate, the pattern ends with the type we have less of\n        # So we can add one more of the type we have more of\n        pairs = min(x, y)\n        excess = max(x, y) - pairs\n        # We can use at most 1 excess string to avoid creating AAA/BBB\n        usable_excess = min(excess, 1)\n        return 2*(pairs + usable_excess) + 2*min(x, y) + 2*z\n```\n\nLet me reconsider once more with the examples:\n\n```python\ndef solve(x, y, z):\n    # We can always use all z \"AB\" strings: 2*z characters\n    \n    # For AA and BB, the optimal strategy is to alternate them\n    # If we alternate AA and BB, we can use min(x,y) of each\n    # After alternating, we can add at most one more AA or BB\n    # This is because adding more would create AAA or BBB\n    \n    pairs = min(x, y)\n    # Total from AA/BB = 2*pairs (from alternating) + 2 extra (at most one more of the abundant type)\n    # When x == y, no extra possible without risk\n    # When x != y, we can add one more of the more abundant type\n    \n    if x == y:\n        # Can use all x AA and y BB through perfect alternation\n        aa_bb_total = 2*x + 2*y\n    else:\n        # Use 2*min(x,y) of both, plus 2 more from the excess (at most 1 extra string)\n        aa_bb_total = 4*min(x, y) + 2\n    \n    return aa_bb_total + 2*z\n```",
