      "generated_answer": "We are given a **complete weighted undirected graph** with $ N $ vertices (numbered 1 to N), and we're to pick a **set of edges** such that:\n\n- No two edges share an endpoint (i.e., chosen edges form a **matching**),\n- We want to **maximize** the total weight.\n\nThis is a classic **Maximum Weight Matching** problem on a general graph. However:\n\n- $ N \\leq 16 $,\n- The graph is **complete**, undirected, and weights are positive,\n- We want **pairwise distinct endpoints**, i.e., a **matching** (each vertex appears in at most one edge),\n- And we want the **maximum total weight**.\n\nSince $ N \\leq 16 $, we can use **bitmask dynamic programming** to solve the **Maximum Weight Matching** in general graphs efficiently.\n\nBut note: because the maximum degree of a vertex in a matching is 1, and the graph is small, we can do a DP over subsets (represented as bit masks), where we:\n\n- Process vertices in order.\n- For the lowest-index unpaired vertex, try matching it with any other unpaired vertex.\n- Use `dp[mask]` = maximum total weight for the subset of vertices represented by `mask` (where 1 means already considered/used).\n\n---\n\n### Plan:\n\nWe will:\n1. Parse input.\n2. Store the weights in a 2D list `D[i][j]` (symmetric).\n3. Use DP with bitmask: `dp[bitmask]` = max total weight using vertices in `bitmask`.\n4. For each state, if the smallest unpaired vertex is `i`, try pairing `i` with any `j > i` that is not used.\n5. Update the state accordingly.\n\n---\n\nLet’s implement:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    \n    n = int(data[0])\n    # Read the upper triangular part: D[i][j] for i < j\n    D = [[0] * n for _ in range(n)]\n    idx = 1\n    for i in range(n-1):\n        for j in range(i+1, n):\n            D[i][j] = int(data[idx])\n            D[j][i] = int(data[idx])\n            idx += 1\n\n    # dp[mask] = maximum total weight for the set of used vertices (mask)\n    dp = [0] * (1 << n)\n    \n    # Iterate over all masks\n    for mask in range(1 << n):\n        # Find the smallest index not in mask\n        i = 0\n        while i < n:\n            if not (mask & (1 << i)):\n                break\n            i += 1\n        # If all vertices are used, skip\n        if i >= n:\n            continue\n        # Try to match vertex i with any j > i not in mask\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n            if not (mask & (1 << j)):\n                new_mask = mask | (1 << i) | (1 << j)\n                new_value = dp[mask] + D[i][j]\n                if new_value > dp[new_mask]:\n                    dp[new_mask] = new_value\n\n    # Answer is dp[(1 << n) - 1], but actually best over all masks\n    # Because we may not be able to use all vertices (if n is odd), so we take max\n    # However, our DP ensures only valid matchings, and we propagate correctly.\n    # Actually, we update all reachable masks, so answer is the max of dp\n    # But since adding edges only increases value (weights >= 1), and we want max,\n    # we can just take dp[(1<<n)-1] OR better: max over all masks?\n    # But our transitions ensure optimality at each mask, and the full matching may not be possible,\n    # but the DP includes partial matchings and builds up.\n\n    # Actually, we don't need max over all: dp is non-decreasing in set inclusion?\n    # Not exactly—but our transitions ensure that larger masks are built from smaller ones.\n    # But since we process in increasing order of mask, and update larger masks, the final answer\n    # is the best matching we can get (possibly not using all vertices).\n\n    # However, note that in our transition, we only add edges, so every new state has two more vertices.\n    # The maximum matching may not use all vertices (if n is odd), and we are already allowing that.\n\n    # So answer = max(dp), but actually we propagate optimally so dp[full] might not be best?\n    # No: consider n=3, dp[111] might not be updated if we match two and leave one.\n    # But we do: we update states with 2, then from a state with 2 we can add another edge only if two more are free.\n    # But in n=3, we can have one edge (two vertices) and leave one. dp[111] would be set only if we had a triangle?\n    # No: we never pick three edges. We only pick disjoint edges.\n\n    # So mask 111 could be reached by first picking (0,1), then pick (2,?) -> no.\n    # Actually, mask 111 cannot be reached by two edges? No: two edges use 4 vertices. Only one or two edges possible.\n\n    # For n=3: we can pick at most one edge (2 vertices) or maybe two edges? No: 3 vertices -> one edge (2 vertices), one left.\n    # So best matching uses at most floor(n/2) edges.\n\n    # Our DP correctly builds states with increasing numbers of used vertices in steps of 2.\n\n    # But we don't need max over all masks: since each transition increases the total weight (positive weights),\n    # and we want the maximum total sum, but a larger matching has more edges and higher sum — but not always: maybe one heavy edge > two light ones?\n\n    # Actually, weights are positive, so more edges = more total weight **if** you could always add an edge.\n    # But you can't. For example, maybe a single heavy edge is better than two medium ones.\n\n    # But in our DP, we consider **all** possible matchings of any size. However, our recurrence:\n\n    # We start from a mask, add one edge (i,j), so we go from mask to mask + i + j.\n\n    # But we do not require to use all vertices. So the best matching might be a partial one.\n\n    # But note: we iterate over all masks, and we update larger masks from smaller ones.\n    # Since all weights are positive, adding an edge increases total weight.\n    # So maximum dp[mask] will be achieved on masks with as many edges as possible? Not necessarily: maybe the only available edge is small?\n\n    # But since all weights are positive, **if** we can add an edge, it would