en the first `#` found might be at (min_i, min_j+1), but the min_i and min_j will still be correct because other `#`s exist around it (since the rectangle is at least 2×2, and only one is missing).\n\nSo even if a corner is missing, the min/max of the remaining `#` will still span the full original rectangle.\n\nThus, this approach is safe.\n\n---\n\n### **Implementation**\n\nWe read H and W, then H lines of strings.\n\nFind `min_i`, `max_i`, `min_j`, `max_j` over all `#`.\n\nThen iterate over the rectangle from `(min_i, min_j)` to `(max_i, max_j)`, find the only `.` in this region — that’s the answer.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Code\n\n```python\ndef find_eaten_cookie():\n    import sys\n    data = sys.stdin.read().splitlines()\n    H, W = map(int, data[0].split())\n    grid = []\n    for i in range(1, 1+H):\n        grid.append(data[i].strip())\n    \n    # Find the bounding box of all '#' \n    min_i, max_i = H, -1\n    min_j, max_j = W, -1\n\n    for i in range(H):\n        for j in range(W):\n            if grid[i][j] == '#':\n                min_i = min(min_i, i)\n                max_i = max(max_i, i)\n                min_j = min(min_j, j)\n                max_j = max(max_j, j)\n    \n    # Now, the original rectangle was [min_i, max_i] x [min_j, max_j]\n    # Find the only '.' in this rectangle\n    for i in range(min_i, max_i + 1):\n        for j in range(min_j, max_j + 1):\n            if grid[i][j] == '.':\n                print(i + 1, j + 1)  # Convert to 1-indexed\n                return\n\nfind_eaten_cookie()\n```",
