    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_194",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nWe have a grid with H rows and W columns.\r\nLet (i,j) denote the square at the i-th row from the top and j-th column from the left.\r\nEach square in the grid is one of the following: the start square, the goal square, an empty square, a wall square, and a candy square.\r\n(i,j) is represented by a character A_{{i,j}}, and is the start square if A_{{i,j}}= S, the goal square if A_{{i,j}}= G, an empty square if A_{{i,j}}= ., a wall square if A_{{i,j}}= #, and a candy square if A_{{i,j}}= o.\r\nHere, it is guaranteed that there are exactly one start, exactly one goal, and at most 18 candy squares.\nTakahashi is now at the start square.\r\nHe can repeat moving to a vertically or horizontally adjacent non-wall square.\r\nHe wants to reach the goal square in at most T moves.\r\nDetermine whether it is possible.\r\nIf it is possible, find the maximum number of candy squares he can visit on the way to the goal square, where he must finish.\r\nEach candy square counts only once, even if it is visited multiple times.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nH W T\r\nA_{{1,1}}A_{{1,2}}\\dots A_{{1,W}}\r\n\\vdots\r\nA_{{H,1}}A_{{H,2}}\\dots A_{{H,W}}\n\nOutput\n\nIf it is impossible to reach the goal square in at most T moves, print -1.\r\nOtherwise, print the maximum number of candy squares that can be visited on the way to the goal square, where Takahashi must finish.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1\\leq H,W \\leq 300\n- 1 \\leq T \\leq 2\\times 10^6\n- H, W, and T are integers.\n- A_{{i,j}} is one of S, G, ., #, and o.\n- Exactly one pair (i,j) satisfies A_{{i,j}}= S.\n- Exactly one pair (i,j) satisfies A_{{i,j}}= G.\n- At most 18 pairs (i,j) satisfy A_{{i,j}}= o.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n3 3 5\r\nS.G\r\no#o\r\n.#.\n\nSample Output 1\n\n1\r\n\nIf he makes four moves as (1,1) \\rightarrow (1,2) \\rightarrow (1,3) \\rightarrow (2,3) \\rightarrow (1,3), he can visit one candy square and finish at the goal square.\r\nHe cannot make five or fewer moves to visit two candy squares and finish at the goal square, so the answer is 1.\nNote that making five moves as (1,1) \\rightarrow (2,1) \\rightarrow (1,1) \\rightarrow (1,2) \\rightarrow (1,3) \\rightarrow (2,3) to visit two candy squares is invalid since he would not finish at the goal square.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n3 3 1\r\nS.G\r\n.#o\r\no#.\n\nSample Output 2\n\n-1\r\n\nHe cannot reach the goal square in one or fewer moves.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n5 10 2000000\r\nS.o..ooo..\r\n..o..o.o..\r\n..o..ooo..\r\n..o..o.o..\r\n..o..ooo.G\n\nSample Output 3\n\n18",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nfrom collections import deque\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().splitlines()\n    \n    H, W, T = map(int, data[0].split())\n    \n    grid = []\n    for i in range(1, H + 1):\n        grid.append(data[i].strip())\n    \n    # Find start, goal and candy positions\n    start_pos = None\n    goal_pos = None\n    candies = []\n    \n    for i in range(H):\n        for j in range(W):\n            if grid[i][j] == 'S':\n                start_pos = (i, j)\n            elif grid[i][j] == 'G':\n                goal_pos = (i, j)\n            elif grid[i][j] == 'o':\n                candies.append((i, j))\n    \n    # Precompute distances between important points (start, goal, candies)\n    points = [start_pos] + candies + [goal_pos]\n    n_points = len(points)\n    dist = [[-1] * n_points for _ in range(n_points)]\n    \n    # Directions for movement\n    directions = [(0, 1), (0, -1), (1, 0), (-1, 0)]\n    \n    def bfs_from_point(r, c):\n        dq = deque([(r, c, 0)])\n        visited = [[False] * W for _ in range(H)]\n        visited[r][c] = True\n        \n        while dq:\n            i, j, d = dq.popleft()\n            # Check if this position is one of our points\n            if (i, j) in points:\n                idx = points.index((i, j))\n                dist[points.index((r, c))][idx] = d\n                # We want distances to all other points, so keep going\n            for di, dj in directions:\n                ni, nj = i + di, j + dj\n                if 0 <= ni < H and 0 <= nj < W and not visited[ni][nj] and grid[ni][nj] != '#':\n                    visited[ni][nj] = True\n                    dq.append((ni, nj, d + 1))\n    \n    # Calculate distances from each point\n    for i, (r, c) in enumerate(points):\n        bfs_from_point(r, c)\n    \n    # Check if start or goal is unreachable\n    if dist[0][n_points - 1] == -1:\n        print(-1)\n        return\n    \n    # If shortest path from start to goal is already more than T, impossible\n    if dist[0][n_points - 1] > T:\n        print(-1)\n        return\n    \n    # Now we use DP with bitmasks for the candies\n    # We have at most 18 candies, so we can use bitmask DP\n    n_candies = len(candies)\n    \n    # If no candies, just check if direct path is possible\n    if n_candies == 0:\n        if dist[0][n_points - 1] <= T:\n            print(0)\n        else:\n            print(- metaData: Title: None and \n- Previous title: None\n- Conflict resolution: None\n        return\n    \n    # dp[mask][i] = minimum time to reach candy i (or start if i == -1) with collected candies in mask\n    # We'll restructure: dp[mask][i] where i is index in candies (0 to n_candies-1), and we'll separately track start and goal\n    INF = float('inf')\n    dp = [[INF] * n_candies for _ in range(1 << n_candies)]\n    \n    # Initialize: from start to each candy\n    for i in range(n_candies):\n        if dist[0][i + 1] != -1:  # start to candy i\n            dp[1 << i][i] = dist[0][i + 1]\n    \n    # Also handle the case where we go straight to goal without any candies\n    best = 0 if dist[0][n_points - 1] <= T else -1\n    \n    # Fill DP table\n    for mask in range(1 << n_candies):\n        for last in range(n_candies):\n            if dp[mask][last] == INF or not (mask & (1 << last)):\n                continue\n            # Try to go to another candy\n            for nxt in range(n_candies):\n                if mask & (1 << nxt):\n                    continue  # already collected\n                if dist[last + 1][nxt + 1] == -1:\n                    continue  # no path\n                new_time = dp[mask][last] + dist[last + 1][nxt + 1]\n                new_mask = mask | (1 << nxt)\n                if new_time < dp[new_mask][nxt]:\n                    dp[new_mask][nxt] = new_time\n    \n    # Check all states: from each state, try to go to goal\n    for mask in range(1 << n_candies):\n        candy_count = bin(mask).count('1')\n        # Count candies\n        for last in range(n_candies):\n            if not (mask & (1 << last)) or dp[mask][last] == INF:\n                continue\n            # From last candy to goal\n            if dist[last + 1][n_points - 1] == -1:\n                continue\n            total_time = dp[mask][last] + dist[last + 1][n_points - 1]\n            if total_time <= T:\n                best = max(best, candy_count)\n        \n        # Also check path from start to goal through some candies in mask, but ending at goal\n        # Actually, we need to consider going from start -> some candies -> goal\n        # We've already covered the case above, but we also need to consider direct start->goal with collecting some candies\n        # No, our DP covers collection paths, but we also need to check start->goal directly with some candies on the way\n        # Actually, we need to check if we can reach any subset of candies and then go to goal\n        \n        # One more case: what if we can go start -> goal directly and collect no candies? (already handled)\n    \n    # One more possibility: we might not need to visit the candies through other candies\n    # We might go start -> candy directly -> goal\n    for i in range(n_candies):\n        if dist[0][i + 1] != -1 and dist[i + 1][n_points - 1] != -1:\n            total_time = dist[0][i + 1] + dist[i + 1][n_points - 1]\n            if total_time <= T:\n                best = max(best, 1)\n    \n    # Final check: is it possible to reach goal at all within T moves?\n    if dist[0][n_points - 1] <= T:\n        print(best)\n    else:\n        print(-1)\n\nsolve()\n```",
