    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given two arrays nums1 and nums2 consisting of positive integers.\nYou have to replace all the 0's in both arrays with strictly positive integers such that the sum of elements of both arrays becomes equal.\nReturn the minimum equal sum you can obtain, or -1 if it is impossible.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums1 = [3,2,0,1,0], nums2 = [6,5,0]\nOutput: 12\nExplanation: We can replace 0's in the following way:\n- Replace the two 0's in nums1 with the values 2 and 4. The resulting array is nums1 = [3,2,2,1,4].\n- Replace the 0 in nums2 with the value 1. The resulting array is nums2 = [6,5,1].\nBoth arrays have an equal sum of 12. It can be shown that it is the minimum sum we can obtain.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums1 = [2,0,2,0], nums2 = [1,4]\nOutput: -1\nExplanation: It is impossible to make the sum of both arrays equal.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= nums1.length, nums2.length <= 10^5\n0 <= nums1[i], nums2[i] <= 10^6",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef minEqualSum(nums1, nums2):\n    sum1 = sum(nums1)\n    sum2 = sum(nums2)\n    zeros1 = nums1.count(0)\n    zeros2 = nums2.count(0)\n    \n    # If one array has zeros but sum is already greater than the other array's max possible sum\n    if zeros1 == 0 and sum1 < sum2 + zeros2:\n        return -1\n    if zeros2 == 0 and sum2 < sum1 + zeros1:\n        return -1\n    \n    # Minimum sum possible for each array\n    min_sum1 = sum1 + zeros1  # Replace each 0 with 1\n    min_sum2 = sum2 + zeros2  # Replace each 0 with 1\n    \n    # The minimum equal sum must be at least the maximum of these minimum sums\n    min_equal_sum = max(min_sum1, min_sum2)\n    \n    # Check if we can achieve this sum with the other array\n    required1 = min_equal_sum - sum1\n    required2 = min_equal_sum - sum2\n    \n    # We need to make sure we can distribute the required sum among the zeros\n    # Each zero must be replaced with at least 1, so we check if we have enough \"extra\"\n    if zeros1 > 0 and required1 < zeros1:  # This shouldn't happen since min_sum1 accounts for this\n        return -1\n    if zeros2 > 0 and required2 < zeros2:\n        return -1\n    \n    return min_equal_sum\n```",
