    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_450",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nTakahashi has N cards from the card game \"AtCoder Magics.\" The i-th card will be called card i. Each card has two parameters: strength and cost. Card i has a strength of A_i and a cost of C_i.\nHe does not like weak cards, so he will discard them. Specifically, he will repeat the following operation until it can no longer be performed:\n\n- Choose two cards x and y such that A_x > A_y and C_x < C_y. Discard card y.\n\nIt can be proved that the set of remaining cards when the operations can no longer be performed is uniquely determined. Find this set of cards.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN\nA_1 C_1\nA_2 C_2\n\\vdots\nA_N C_N\n\nOutput\n\nLet there be m remaining cards, cards i_1, i_2, \\dots, i_m, in ascending order. Print these in the following format:\nm\ni_1 i_2 \\cdots i_m\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 2 \\leq N \\leq 2 \\times 10^5\n- 1 \\leq A_i, C_i \\leq 10^9\n- A_1, A_2, \\dots ,A_N are all distinct.\n- C_1, C_2, \\dots ,C_N are all distinct.\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n3\n2 4\n1 1\n3 2\n\nSample Output 1\n\n2\n2 3\n\nFocusing on cards 1 and 3, we have A_1 < A_3 and C_1 > C_3, so card 1 can be discarded.\nNo further operations can be performed. At this point, cards 2 and 3 remain, so print them.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n5\n1 1\n10 2\n100 3\n1000 4\n10000 5\n\nSample Output 2\n\n5\n1 2 3 4 5\n\nIn this case, no cards can be discarded.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n6\n32 101\n65 78\n2 29\n46 55\n103 130\n52 40\n\nSample Output 3\n\n4\n2 3 5 6",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve():\n    import sys\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().split()\n    \n    n = int(data[0])\n    cards = []\n    for i in range(n):\n        a = int(data[1 + 2 * i])\n        c = int(data[1 + 2 * i + 1])\n        cards.append((a, c, i + 1))  # (strength, cost, original_index)\n    \n    # Sort cards by strength in ascending order\n    cards.sort()\n    \n    # We'll maintain a list of remaining cards\n    # A card should be discarded if there exists a stronger card with lower cost\n    # We traverse from weakest to strongest and maintain the minimum cost seen so far among stronger cards\n    remaining = []\n    \n    # We process cards in increasing order of strength\n    # For each card, we check if there's a previous card with lower cost (which would be stronger since we're going in increasing strength)\n    # Actually, let's use a different approach: \n    # A card i should be kept only if there is no other card j with A_j > A_i and C_j < C_i\n    # Alternatively, we can think of this as: we keep a card if it's not dominated by any other card\n    # Card x dominates card y if A_x > A_y and C_x < C_y\n    # We want to find all cards that are not dominated by any other card\n    \n    # Sort by strength ascending, and if same strength, by cost descending (but strengths are distinct)\n    cards.sort()\n    \n    # Now traverse from highest strength to lowest\n    # For each card, check if it's dominated by any card with higher strength\n    # But we can do better: we can use a monotonic stack approach\n    \n    # Let's sort by strength ascending and then traverse backwards\n    cards.sort()  # by strength ascending\n    remaining_candidates = []\n    \n    # Traverse from strongest to weakest\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        a, c, idx = cards[i]\n        # Check if this card is dominated by any card in remaining_candidates\n        # Since we're going from strongest to weakest, all cards in remaining_candidates are stronger\n        # So we only need to check if any card in remaining_candidates has cost < c\n        # But we need to be careful: we want to keep a card if there's no stronger card with lower cost\n        # Actually, if there exists a card in remaining_candidates with lower cost, then current card is dominated\n        dominated = False\n        for ra, rc, ridx in remaining_candidates:\n            if rc < c:  # stronger card (since we're processing backwards) with lower cost\n                dominated = True\n                break\n        \n        if not dominated:\n            remaining_candidates.append((a, c, idx))\n    \n    # The remaining cards are those not dominated\n    # Sort by original index for output\n    result_indices = sorted([card[2] for card in remaining_candidates])\n    \n    print(len(result_indices))\n    if result_indices:\n        print(' '.join(map(str, result_indices)))\n\nsolve()\n```",
