      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to:\n\n1. Generate all possible subsequences of length k from the array\n2. For each subsequence, calculate its \"power\" which is the minimum absolute difference between any two elements\n3. Sum up all these powers and return the result modulo 10^9 + 7\n\nLet me trace through the examples to understand:\n- Example 1: nums = [1,2,3,4], k = 3\n  - [1,2,3]: min(|1-2|, |1-3|, |2-3|) = min(1,2,1) = 1\n  - [1,3,4]: min(|1-3|, |1-4|, |3-4|) = min(2,3,1) = 1  \n  - [1,2,4]: min(|1-2|, |1-4|, |2-4|) = min(1,3,2) = 1\n  - [2,3,4]: min(|2-3|, |2-4|, |3-4|) = min(1,2,1) = 1\n  - Sum = 1+1+1+1 = 4\n\nWait, let me check the explanation again. It says: |2-3| + |3-4| + |2-1| + |3-4| = 1+1+1+1 = 4.\nActually looking more carefully, the explanation seems to be calculating the minimum difference incorrectly. Let me reconsider.\n\nActually, the power is the minimum absolute difference between any two elements in the subsequence. For [1,2,3], the differences are |1-2|=1, |1-3|=2, |2-3|=1, so minimum is 1.\n\nBut the calculation in the explanation appears to be using specific pairs. Let me re-read: \"The sum of powers is |2 - 3| + |3 - 4| + |2 - 1| + |3 - 4| = 4.\"\n\nI think there might be an error in the explanation, but the logic should be:\n- For each subsequence of length k, find the minimum absolute difference among all pairs\n- Sum these minimum values\n\nFor efficiency, I can sort the array first, since the minimum difference in a subsequence will always be between adjacent elements when sorted.\n\n```python\ndef sumOfPowers(nums, k):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    n = len(nums)\n    nums.sort()\n    \n    def get_min_diff(subseq):\n        if len(subseq) < 2:\n            return float('inf')\n        min_diff = float('inf')\n        for i in range(len(subseq)):\n            for j in range(i + 1, len(subseq)):\n                min_diff = min(min_diff, abs(subseq[i] - subseq[j]))\n        return min_diff\n    \n    def backtrack(start, current_subseq):\n        if len(current_subseq) == k:\n            return get_min_diff(current_subseq)\n        \n        if start >= n or len(current_subseq) + (n - start) < k:\n            return 0\n            \n        total = 0\n        # Include nums[start]\n        current_subseq.append(nums[start])\n        total = (total + backtrack(start + 1, current_subseq)) % MOD\n        current_subseq.pop()\n        \n        # Exclude nums[start]\n        total = (total + backtrack(start + 1, current_subseq)) % MOD\n        \n        return total\n    \n    return backtrack(0, [])\n```",
