`k` ones, using:\n\n- Existing 1s (prioritize closer ones).\n- Up to `maxChanges` created 1s (cheaper than long swaps).\n\nBut we can optimize by noting:\n\n- The optimal `aliceIndex` is likely near where the 1s are clustered.\n- The cost of swapping a 1 from position `i` to `j` is `|i - j|`.\n\nHence, we need to:\n1. Precompute positions of 1s.\n2. For each `aliceIndex` in `[0, n-1]`, calculate:\n   - How many 1s are already available nearby.\n   - Use a window of up to `k` ones (from existing and possibly `maxChanges` created).\n   - Cost = sum of distances (via prefix sums).\n   - If fewer than `k` existing 1s can be efficiently brought, top up with created 1s (via change, but they can be placed at `aliceIndex ± 1`, etc., so cheap to move).\n\nHowever, note: **change** allows setting `nums[j] = 1` for any `j`, so if we want to create a 1 to bring to Alice, we should create it **as close as possible** (ideally at `aliceIndex ± 1`, then one move to bring in).\n\nBut we can even create it **at adjacent index** → cost **1** to bring in.\n\nSo:\n- **Creating a 1 at adjacent position → cost 1 move to bring to Alice**.\n- **Total cost for such a created-and-swapped 1:** 2 moves (1 to create, 1 to swap in), **but note:** we can create and swap multiple times.\n\nBut wait: in **example 2**, creating a 1 at index 1 (from Alice at 0), then swapping from 1→0: cost = 2 per 1. They did 2 such ones: total cost 4.\n\nBut observe:\n- Each 1 that is **created far away** is expensive to bring in.\n- Best: **create 1s adjacent to Alice**, so only 1 move to swap in.\n\nSo: **created 1s cost 1 (create) + 1 (swap) = 2** per 1, but **only if we create them at adjacent index**.\n\nHence, we can simulate:\n- Use existing 1s around Alice (cost = distance).\n- For extra ones (up to `maxChanges`), we can “get” them in 2 moves each (create adjacent, swap in), **but only if not already used**.\n\nBut wait: is it worth creating a 1 at distance if cheaper than bringing a far existing 1?\n\nFor example, if a 1 is 5 away → cost 5. Creating a new one costs 2. So better to create.\n\nSo **rule**:\n- Use up to `maxChanges` **created 1s**, each costing **2 moves**.\n- For remaining ones, pick closest existing 1s, cost = distance to Alice.\n\nBut we **cannot create** a 1 at Alice's index (because condition says `j != aliceIndex`).\n\nSo must place at adjacent or farther.\n\nBut to minimize swap cost, place at adjacent → cost = 1.\n\nHence, total for created 1: 1 (create) + 1 (swap) = 2.\n\nSo:\n- **Each created 1 adds 2 to total move count.**\n- For existing 1s, cost is the number of swaps = distance from position to `aliceIndex`.\n\nBut what if there’s a long gap? We can only swap adjacent, so cost = `|i - aliceIndex|`.\n\nBut note: multiple swaps can happen in parallel in logic, but each swap counts.\n\nSo:\n\n### Total cost if Alice starts at `i`:\nLet:\n- Let `ones = list of indices where nums[j] == 1`\n- We want to collect `k` ones.\n\nWe can use:\n- Up to `maxChanges` ones created at cost **2 each**.\n- The rest must come from **existing 1s**, at cost `|j - i|` each.\n\nBut **we can skip distant existing 1s** and use created ones if cheaper.\n\nSo:\n- We will prefer **existing 1s with cost < 2** → only those at distance 0 or 1.\n  - Distance 0: already at Alice (free, if exists).\n  - Distance 1: cost 1 < 2 → better than creating.\n\nSo, **strategy per `aliceIndex = i`**:\n1. **Immediate pickup**: if `nums[i] == 1`, then one free pickup (counted, but 0 cost).\n2. We need `k` total ones.\n3. Available existing 1s: all positions where `nums[j] == 1` and `j` ≠ `i` or we can still use even if used before — wait, each 1 can be used once.\n\nSo better: collect all positions of 1s.\n\nLet’s define:\n- Let `positions` be all indices where `nums[j] == 1`.\n\nFor a fixed `aliceIndex = i`, define:\n- Let `distances = sorted([abs(j - i) for j in positions])` — cost to bring each 1 to `i`.\n- We can use up to `maxChanges` created 1s, each cost 2.\n- We want minimum total cost to get `k` ones.\n\nLet `needed = k`.\nIf `nums[i] == 1`, then we already have one → `needed -= 1`.\n\nNow, we can:\n- Pick up to `min(maxChanges, needed)` **created** ones, each costs 2.\n- The rest from existing distant 1s.\n\nBut we can **choose** which existing 1s to use. We want the **cheapest ones (shortest distance)**.\n\nSo:\n- Sort distances.\n- Precompute prefix sums so we can quickly compute cost of using first `t` existing 1s (excluding the one at `i` if any? No — include all, but if `i` has a 1, we already used it for free).\n\nBut note: if there is a 1 at `i`, it's already collected, but if we include `i` in `positions`, then `abs(i - i) = 0`, so it would come first — good.\n\nSo we don’t need to special-case `nums[i]` — just include it and sort by distance.\n\nBut caution: can we use the 1 at `i`? Yes, and it costs 0 swaps. But we **only get to collect it at start** — and it disappears.\n\nSo in modeling: if we include the 1 at `aliceIndex`, its cost is 0 — correct.\n\nSo plan:\n\nFor each `aliceIndex` in `[0, n-1]`:\n1. `needed = k`\n2. Collect distances from all 1s to `aliceIndex`: `d_j = |j - aliceIndex|`\n3. Sort those distances → `d_sorted`\n4. We are allowed to use **existing** 1s with cost = distance.\n   But we can also **skip** an existing 1 (if far) and use a **created 1** at cost 2 per 1.\n",
