    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_56",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a 0-indexed integer array nums. A subarray s of length m is called alternating if:\n\nm is greater than 1.\ns_1 = s_0 + 1.\nThe 0-indexed subarray s looks like [s_0, s_1, s_0, s_1,...,s_(m-1) % 2]. In other words, s_1 - s_0 = 1, s_2 - s_1 = -1, s_3 - s_2 = 1, s_4 - s_3 = -1, and so on up to s[m - 1] - s[m - 2] = (-1)^m.\n\nReturn the maximum length of all alternating subarrays present in nums or -1 if no such subarray exists.\nA subarray is a contiguous non-empty sequence of elements within an array.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [2,3,4,3,4]\nOutput: 4\nExplanation: The alternating subarrays are [3,4], [3,4,3], and [3,4,3,4]. The longest of these is [3,4,3,4], which is of length 4.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [4,5,6]\nOutput: 2\nExplanation: [4,5] and [5,6] are the only two alternating subarrays. They are both of length 2.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n2 <= nums.length <= 100\n1 <= nums[i] <= 10^4",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to find the maximum length of alternating subarrays.\n\nLet me analyze the pattern:\n- For a subarray to be alternating: `s_1 = s_0 + 1`\n- The differences alternate: `+1, -1, +1, -1, ...`\n- So if we have elements `[a, a+1, a, a+1, a, ...]`\n\nKey insights:\n1. The subarray must start with two consecutive elements where the second is exactly one more than the first\n2. After that, elements alternate between the starting value and starting value + 1\n3. I need to check every possible starting position and extend as far as possible\n\nMy approach:\n1. Iterate through each possible starting position\n2. For each position, check if it can start an alternating subarray (nums[i+1] == nums[i] + 1)\n3. If yes, extend the subarray as far as possible while maintaining the alternating pattern\n4. Track the maximum length found\n\n```python\ndef maxLengthAlternatingSubarray(nums):\n    max_length = 0\n    n = len(nums)\n    \n    for i in range(n - 1):\n        # Check if we can start an alternating subarray at position i\n        if nums[i + 1] != nums[i] + 1:\n            continue\n            \n        # We have the first two elements satisfying s1 = s0 + 1\n        current_length = 2\n        expected_next = nums[i]  # After [a, a+1], we expect a, then a+1, etc.\n        \n        # Extend the subarray as far as possible\n        for j in range(i + 2, n):\n            if nums[j] == expected_next:\n                current_length += 1\n                # Alternate the expected value for next iteration\n                expected_next = nums[i] + 1 if expected_next == nums[i] else nums[i]\n            else:\n                break\n                \n        max_length = max(max_length, current_length)\n    \n    return max_length if max_length > 0 else -1\n```",
