    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef minSizeSubarray(nums, target):\n    n = len(nums)\n    total_sum = sum(nums)\n    \n    # If target is larger than total_sum, we need to consider full cycles\n    # plus a partial subarray\n    full_cycles = target // total_sum\n    remainder = target % total_sum\n    \n    # If remainder is 0, then we can achieve target with full cycles\n    if remainder == 0:\n        return full_cycles * n\n    \n    # Use sliding window to find shortest subarray with sum = remainder\n    # We need to check at most 2*n elements to cover all possible wrapping cases\n    double_nums = nums + nums\n    min_length = float('inf')\n    \n    left = 0\n    current_sum = 0\n    \n    for right in range(len(double_nums)):\n        current_sum += double_nums[right]\n        \n        # Shrink window from left while sum is greater than remainder\n        while current_sum > remainder and left <= right:\n            current_sum -= double_nums[left]\n            left += 1\n        \n        # Check if we found a subarray with sum equal to remainder\n        if current_sum == remainder:\n            min_length = min(min_length, right - left + 1)\n            \n        # If the window becomes too large, we can continue\n        # (this optimization helps avoid unnecessary iterations)\n    \n    # If no valid subarray was found for the remainder\n    if min_length == float('inf'):\n        return -1\n    \n    # Total length is full_cycles * n + length of subarray with sum = remainder\n    return full_cycles * n + min_length\n```",
