      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve():\n    N = int(input().strip())\n    \n    # We need to find the maximum palindromic cube number <= N\n    # A palindromic cube number is a perfect cube whose value is a palindrome\n    \n    # Since N can be up to 10^18, the cube root of N is up to 10^6\n    # So we can iterate from cube root of N downwards\n    \n    max_palindromic_cube = 1  # At minimum, 1^3 = 1 is always a palindromic cube\n    \n    # Find the maximum x such that x^3 <= N\n    # We can use binary search or approximate\n    left, right = 1, int(N**(1/3)) + 2\n    while left <= right:\n        mid = (left + right) // 2\n        if mid ** 3 <= N:\n            x_max = mid\n            left = mid + 1\n        else:\n            right = mid - 1\n    \n    # Now check all cubes from x_max down to 1\n    for x in range(x_max, 0, -1):\n        cube = x ** 3\n        if cube > N:\n            continue\n            \n        # Check if cube is a palindrome\n        cube_str = str(cube)\n        if cube_str == cube_str[::-1]:\n            max_palindromic_cube = cube\n            break\n    \n    print(max_palindromic_cube)\n\nsolve()\n```",
