ting with 2\n\nActually, when we choose index 1, we're deleting the occurrence at index 2 (the closest to the right), but we don't delete the character at the chosen index. So we started with 2 'b's and ended with 1 'b'.\n\nThe pattern seems to be: for each character, we can reduce its count by removing pairs of occurrences, but one might remain.\n\nActually, the key insight is simpler: each operation removes at most 2 characters (one from left, one from right of the chosen position). But the chosen character itself remains.\n\nAfter reading the problem again, I think the solution is that we can always reduce each character's frequency to at most 1, because we can pair up occurrences. The minimum length is the number of distinct characters that appear an odd number of times.\n\nBut looking at the examples:\n- \"aaabc\": a(3, odd), b(1, odd), c(1, odd) → 3 characters with odd count → answer 3 ✓\n- \"cbbd\": c(1, odd), b(2, even), d(1, odd) → 2 characters with odd count → but answer is 3\n\nWait, that doesn't work.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe the answer is simply the number of distinct characters? But \"cbbd\" has 3 distinct chars (c,b,d) → 3 ✓, \"aaabc\" has 3 distinct → 3 ✓, \"dddaaa\" has 2 distinct → 2 ✓.\n\nThis matches all examples!\n\nThe pattern appears to be that no matter what operations we do, we can never eliminate a character type completely if it exists, but we can reduce each character type to appear exactly once.\n\nActually, let me verify with Example 3: \"dddaaa\" → \"da\" has both 'd' and 'a', so 2 distinct characters.\n\nThe insight is that we can always reduce any string to contain at most one occurrence of each character type through these operations, and we can't eliminate a character type completely if it was present initially.\n\nTherefore, the minimized length equals the number of distinct characters in the original string.\n\n```python\ndef minimizedStringLength(s):\n    return len(set(s))\n```",
