    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_142",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a 0-indexed array of positive integers nums and a positive integer limit.\nIn one operation, you can choose any two indices i and j and swap nums[i] and nums[j] if |nums[i] - nums[j]| <= limit.\nReturn the lexicographically smallest array that can be obtained by performing the operation any number of times.\nAn array a is lexicographically smaller than an array b if in the first position where a and b differ, array a has an element that is less than the corresponding element in b. For example, the array [2,10,3] is lexicographically smaller than the array [10,2,3] because they differ at index 0 and 2 < 10.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [1,5,3,9,8], limit = 2\nOutput: [1,3,5,8,9]\nExplanation: Apply the operation 2 times:\n- Swap nums[1] with nums[2]. The array becomes [1,3,5,9,8]\n- Swap nums[3] with nums[4]. The array becomes [1,3,5,8,9]\nWe cannot obtain a lexicographically smaller array by applying any more operations.\nNote that it may be possible to get the same result by doing different operations.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [1,7,6,18,2,1], limit = 3\nOutput: [1,6,7,18,1,2]\nExplanation: Apply the operation 3 times:\n- Swap nums[1] with nums[2]. The array becomes [1,6,7,18,2,1]\n- Swap nums[0] with nums[4]. The array becomes [2,6,7,18,1,1]\n- Swap nums[0] with nums[5]. The array becomes [1,6,7,18,1,2]\nWe cannot obtain a lexicographically smaller array by applying any more operations.\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: nums = [1,7,28,19,10], limit = 3\nOutput: [1,7,28,19,10]\nExplanation: [1,7,28,19,10] is the lexicographically smallest array we can obtain because we cannot apply the operation on any two indices.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= nums.length <= 10^5\n1 <= nums[i] <= 10^9\n1 <= limit <= 10^9",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to find the lexicographically smallest array possible by swapping elements when their absolute difference is ≤ limit.\n\nThe key insight is that elements can be swapped if they're within the limit of each other, and this creates connected components of swappable elements. Within each connected component, we can rearrange the elements in any order (since we can perform any number of swaps).\n\nMy approach:\n1. Sort the array along with original indices\n2. Group elements into connected components where consecutive elements in sorted order have difference ≤ limit\n3. For each connected component, sort the original indices and place the sorted values in those positions\n4. This gives us the lexicographically smallest arrangement\n\n```python\ndef lexicographically_smallest_array(nums, limit):\n    n = len(nums)\n    \n    # Create list of (value, original_index) and sort by value\n    sorted_pairs = sorted([(nums[i], i) for i in range(n)])\n    \n    result = [0] * n\n    \n    i = 0\n    while i < n:\n        # Start a new component\n        component_values = []\n        component_indices = []\n        \n        # Build the current connected component\n        j = i\n        while j < n:\n            component_values.append(sorted_pairs[j][0])\n            component_indices.append(sorted_pairs[j][1])\n            \n            # If we're at the end or the next element is too far away, break\n            if j == n - 1 or sorted_pairs[j + 1][0] - sorted_pairs[j][0] > limit:\n                break\n            j += 1\n        \n        # Sort the indices of this component to get lexicographically smallest arrangement\n        component_indices.sort()\n        \n        # Place the values from this component into the sorted indices\n        for idx, val in zip(component_indices, component_values):\n            result[idx] = val\n        \n        i = j + 1\n    \n    return result\n```",
