      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to count the number of alternating subarrays in a binary array.\n\nAn alternating subarray means no two adjacent elements have the same value.\n\nLet me analyze the examples:\n- For nums = [0,1,1,1]: The alternating subarrays are [0], [1], [1], [1], and [0,1]. This gives us 5 total.\n- For nums = [1,0,1,0]: Every possible subarray is alternating, so we get 10 total.\n\nMy approach:\n1. I'll use a sliding window approach to find contiguous alternating subarrays\n2. When I find a contiguous alternating segment of length n, it contributes n*(n+1)/2 subarrays\n3. I'll iterate through the array and extend the current alternating subarray as long as adjacent elements are different\n\nHere's my solution:\n\n```python\ndef countAlternatingSubarrays(nums):\n    if not nums:\n        return 0\n    \n    total = 0\n    start = 0\n    \n    for end in range(len(nums)):\n        # If we're not at the start and current element equals previous element,\n        # the alternating sequence breaks\n        if end > 0 and nums[end] == nums[end - 1]:\n            # Count all subarrays in the previous alternating segment\n            length = end - start\n            total += length * (length + 1) // 2\n            # Start a new segment\n            start = end\n        \n        # For the last segment or when we reach the end\n        if end == len(nums) - 1:\n            length = end - start + 1\n            total += length * (length + 1) // 2\n    \n    return total\n```",
