    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to count the number of special permutations where adjacent elements are divisible by each other (either a % b == 0 or b % a == 0).\n\nThis is a dynamic programming problem with bitmasks since the constraints are small (n <= 14). I'll use DP with state (mask, last) where:\n- mask represents which elements have been used (bitmask)\n- last is the index of the last element in the current permutation\n\nFor each state, I'll try to extend the permutation by adding any unused element that satisfies the divisibility condition with the last element.\n\n```python\ndef specialPerm(nums):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    n = len(nums)\n    \n    # Precompute which pairs satisfy the divisibility condition\n    valid = [[False] * n for _ in range(n)]\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(n):\n            if i != j:\n                valid[i][j] = (nums[i] % nums[j] == 0) or (nums[j] % nums[i] == 0)\n    \n    # dp[mask][last] = number of ways to arrange elements in mask with last element at index 'last'\n    dp = {}\n    \n    def solve(mask, last):\n        if mask == (1 << n) - 1:  # All elements used\n            return 1\n        \n        if (mask, last) in dp:\n            return dp[(mask, last)]\n        \n        result = 0\n        for next_idx in range(n):\n            if not (mask & (1 << next_idx)) and (last == -1 or valid[last][next_idx]):\n                result = (result + solve(mask | (1 << next_idx), next_idx)) % MOD\n        \n        dp[(mask, last)] = result\n        return result\n    \n    return solve(0, -1)\n```",
