    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a 0-indexed binary string s of length n on which you can apply two types of operations:\n\nChoose an index i and invert all characters from index 0 to index i (both inclusive), with a cost of i + 1\nChoose an index i and invert all characters from index i to index n - 1 (both inclusive), with a cost of n - i\n\nReturn the minimum cost to make all characters of the string equal.\nInvert a character means if its value is '0' it becomes '1' and vice-versa.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: s = \"0011\"\nOutput: 2\nExplanation: Apply the second operation with i = 2 to obtain s = \"0000\" for a cost of 2. It can be shown that 2 is the minimum cost to make all characters equal.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: s = \"010101\"\nOutput: 9\nExplanation: Apply the first operation with i = 2 to obtain s = \"101101\" for a cost of 3.\nApply the first operation with i = 1 to obtain s = \"011101\" for a cost of 2. \nApply the first operation with i = 0 to obtain s = \"111101\" for a cost of 1. \nApply the second operation with i = 4 to obtain s = \"111110\" for a cost of 2.\nApply the second operation with i = 5 to obtain s = \"111111\" for a cost of 1. \nThe total cost to make all characters equal is 9. It can be shown that 9 is the minimum cost to make all characters equal.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= s.length == n <= 10^5\ns[i] is either '0' or '1'",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to find the minimum cost to make all characters in a binary string equal using two types of operations.\n\nLet me analyze the key insights:\n1. The final string will be either all '0's or all '1's\n2. The operations flip prefixes or suffixes\n3. I need to find the optimal sequence of operations\n\nThe key insight is that this problem can be solved using dynamic programming. When we process the string from left to right, we can keep track of the minimum cost to make the current prefix all 0s or all 1s, considering whether we've flipped an even or odd number of times.\n\nFor each position, I need to consider:\n- The cost to make the current prefix all 0s\n- The cost to make the current prefix all 1s\n- Whether the current state requires an even or odd number of flips\n\n```python\ndef minimumCost(s):\n    n = len(s)\n    # dp0: min cost to make current prefix all 0s\n    # dp1: min cost to make current prefix all 1s\n    dp0 = 0\n    dp1 = 0\n    \n    for i in range(n - 1):\n        if s[i] == s[i + 1]:\n            # No change needed, costs remain the same\n            continue\n        else:\n            # Characters differ, need to flip either left or right part\n            # We can flip the suffix starting at i+1, or flip prefix ending at i\n            new_dp0 = min(dp0, dp1) + (i + 1) + (n - (i + 1))\n            new_dp1 = min(dp0, dp1) + (i + 1) + (n - (i + 1))\n            \n            # Actually, we need to be more careful about which one becomes 0 and which becomes 1\n            # If we flip suffix starting at i+1, cost is n-(i+1)\n            # If we flip prefix ending at i, cost is i+1\n            # The optimal is to choose the cheaper operation\n            \n            cost = min(i + 1, n - (i + 1))\n            dp0, dp1 = dp1 + cost, dp0 + cost\n    \n    # Return minimum cost to make entire string all 0s or all 1s\n    return min(dp0, dp1)\n```",
