swer is always ceil(frequency / 3) except when frequency = 1?\n\nLet me check:\n- freq = 2: ceil(2/3) = 1 ✓\n- freq = 3: ceil(3/3) = 1 ✓  \n- freq = 4: ceil(4/3) = 2 ✓ (2+2)\n- freq = 5: ceil(5/3) = 2 ✓ (3+2)\n- freq = 6: ceil(6/3) = 2 ✓ (3+3)\n- freq = 7: ceil(7/3) = 3 ✓ (3+2+2)\n\nBut wait, is there an exception? What about frequency = 1? That's impossible.\n\nActually, I need to be careful:\n- If frequency % 3 == 0: freq // 3\n- If frequency % 3 == 1: if freq == 1: impossible, else (freq - 4) // 3 + 2\n- If frequency % 3 == 2: freq // 3 + 1\n\nBut (freq - 4) // 3 + 2 = freq // 3 + 1 when freq % 3 == 1 and freq > 1.\nLet me verify: freq = 4: (4-4)//3 + 2 = 0 + 2 = 2, and 4//3 + 1 = 1 + 1 = 2 ✓\nfreq = 7: (7-4)//3 + 2 = 1 + 2 = 3, and 7//3 + 1 = 2 + 1 = 3 ✓\n\nActually, for any freq > 1: the result is (freq + 2) // 3, except when we need to check for impossibility.\n\nWait: (freq + 2) // 3:\n- freq = 2: (4)//3 = 1 ✓\n- freq = 3: (5)//3 = 1 ✓  \n- freq = 4: (6)//3 = 2 ✓\n- freq = 5: (7)//3 = 2 ✓\n- freq = 6: (8)//3 = 2 ✓\n- freq = 7: (9)//3 = 3 ✓\n\nBut this works only if we can achieve it. The only impossible case is when frequency = 1.\n\nActually, any frequency ≥ 2 can be reduced using combinations of 2 and 3:\n- This is possible due to the Coin Problem - with denominations 2 and 3 (which are coprime), any amount ≥ 2 can be made, except we need to verify.\n\nSince gcd(2,3) = 1, by Frobenius coin problem, the largest unmakeable amount is 2*3 - 2 - 3 = 1. So any amount ≥ 2 can be made. So only frequency = 1 is impossible.\n\nTherefore, the algorithm:\n1. Count frequencies\n2. For each frequency:\n   - If frequency == 1: return -1\n   - Else: add (frequency + 2) // 3 to result\n\nWait, is (frequency + 2) // 3 the minimum number of operations?\n- We want to minimize the number of operations, which means maximize the sum of (2a + 3b) = frequency, minimize (a + b)\n- Since 3 > 2, we want to maximize b (use as many 3's as possible)\n- So: b = frequency // 3\n- remainder = frequency % 3\n- If remainder == 0: ops = b\n- If remainder == 1: we need to reduce b by 1 and use two 2's instead, so ops = (b-1) + 2 = b + 1\n- If remainder == 2: ops = b + 1\n\nSo: \n- if frequency == 1: impossible\n- else if frequency % 3 == 0: frequency // 3\n- else: frequency // 3 + 1\n\nThis is equivalent to (frequency + 2) // 3 for frequency > 1.\n\nLet me verify:\n- freq 2: (2+2)//3 = 1, and 2//3 + 1 = 0 + 1 = 1 ✓\n- freq 3: (3+2)//3 = 1, and 3//3 = 1 ✓  \n- freq 4: (4+2)//3 = 2, and since 4%3=1, we need (4//3 + 1) = 1 + 1 = 2 ✓\n- freq 5: (5+2)//3 = 2, and 5%3=2, so 5//3 + 1 = 1 + 1 = 2 ✓\n\nPerfect.\n\n```python\ndef minOperations(nums):\n    from collections import Counter\n    \n    freq_count = Counter(nums)\n    total_operations = 0\n    \n    for freq in freq_count.values():\n        if freq == 1:\n            return -1\n        # For any frequency >= 2, minimum operations is ceil(freq/3) \n        # which equals (freq + 2) // 3\n        total_operations += (freq + 2) // 3\n    \n    return total_operations\n```",
