      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to find the minimum value of |max(nums1) - min(nums2)| when partitioning the array into two non-empty parts.\n\nLet me analyze the examples:\n- For [1,3,2,4]: sorted is [1,2,3,4], optimal partition gives value 1\n- For [100,1,10]: sorted is [1,10,100], optimal partition gives value 9\n\nThe key insight is that to minimize |max(nums1) - min(nums2)|, I should sort the array and consider all possible ways to split it. The optimal partition will occur when I cut between two adjacent elements in the sorted array.\n\nWhen I sort the array, the minimum difference will be achieved by placing the first k elements in one group and the remaining elements in the other group, for some k. The value will be |largest in first group - smallest in second group|, which is simply the difference between two adjacent elements in the sorted array.\n\n```python\ndef findPartitionValue(nums):\n    nums.sort()\n    min_diff = float('inf')\n    \n    for i in range(1, len(nums)):\n        diff = abs(nums[i] - nums[i-1])\n        min_diff = min(min_diff, diff)\n    \n    return min_diff\n```",
