    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given three strings s1, s2, and s3. You have to perform the following operation on these three strings as many times as you want.\nIn one operation you can choose one of these three strings such that its length is at least 2 and delete the rightmost character of it.\nReturn the minimum number of operations you need to perform to make the three strings equal if there is a way to make them equal, otherwise, return -1.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: s1 = \"abc\", s2 = \"abb\", s3 = \"ab\"\nOutput: 2\nExplanation: Performing operations on s1 and s2 once will lead to three equal strings.\nIt can be shown that there is no way to make them equal with less than two operations.\nExample 2:\n\nInput: s1 = \"dac\", s2 = \"bac\", s3 = \"cac\"\nOutput: -1\nExplanation: Because the leftmost letters of s1 and s2 are not equal, they could not be equal after any number of operations. So the answer is -1.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= s1.length, s2.length, s3.length <= 100\ns1, s2 and s3 consist only of lowercase English letters.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to:\n\n1. Find if there's a way to make all three strings equal by repeatedly removing the rightmost character from any string\n2. If possible, return the minimum number of operations needed\n3. If impossible, return -1\n\nKey insights:\n- We can only remove characters from the right (we can't add or change characters)\n- The final strings will be prefixes of the original strings\n- For the strings to be equal, they must have a common prefix that all three strings share from position 0\n- The answer is -1 if the very first characters don't match (as shown in Example 2)\n- The minimum operations is the total number of characters we need to remove from all strings\n\nHere's my approach:\n1. Find the longest common prefix that all three strings share\n2. If there's no common prefix (first characters differ), return -1\n3. Otherwise, calculate the operations needed as (len1 - common_prefix_len) + (len2 - common_prefix_len) + (len3 - common_prefix_len)\n\n```python\ndef solve(s1, s2, s3):\n    # Check if first characters are the same - if not, impossible\n    if not s1 or not s2 or not s3 or s1[0] != s2[0] or s1[0] != s3[0]:\n        return -1\n    \n    # Find the length of the longest common prefix\n    min_len = min(len(s1), len(s2), len(s3))\n    common_prefix_len = 0\n    \n    for i in range(min_len):\n        if s1[i] == s2[i] == s3[i]:\n            common_prefix_len += 1\n        else:\n            break\n    \n    # Calculate total operations needed\n    operations = (len(s1) - common_prefix_len) + (len(s2) - common_prefix_len) + (len(s3) - common_prefix_len)\n    \n    return operations\n```",
