    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to find the number of ways to split a binary array into subarrays where each subarray contains exactly one element with value 1.\n\nLet me analyze the examples:\n- For [0,1,0,0,1], I need to split so each subarray has exactly one 1\n- The 1's are at positions 1 and 4 (0-indexed)\n- Each subarray must contain exactly one 1, so I need to make a split between the two 1's\n- The key insight is that I can choose where to make the split between the consecutive 1's\n\nThe strategy:\n1. First, if there are no 1's, it's impossible to have good subarrays (each must contain exactly one 1), so return 0\n2. If there's exactly one 1, there's exactly one way to split (the entire array as one subarray)\n3. If there are multiple 1's, I need to consider the gaps between consecutive 1's\n4. For each gap between consecutive 1's (positions i and j where nums[i] = nums[j] = 1 and i < j), I can make a split at any 0 between them\n5. If there are k zeros between two consecutive 1's, I have (k + 1) choices of where to split\n6. The total number of ways is the product of the number of choices for each gap\n\n```python\ndef numberOfGoodSubarraySplits(nums):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    \n    # Find positions of all 1's\n    ones = []\n    for i in range(len(nums)):\n        if nums[i] == 1:\n            ones.append(i)\n    \n    # If no 1's, impossible to have good subarrays\n    if len(ones) == 0:\n        return 0\n    \n    # If only one 1, only one way to split\n    if len(ones) == 1:\n        return 1\n    \n    # For multiple 1's, calculate the product of (zeros_between + 1) for each consecutive pair\n    result = 1\n    for i in range(1, len(ones)):\n        # Number of positions where we can split between ones[i-1] and ones[i]\n        # This equals the number of zeros between them plus 1\n        zeros_between = ones[i] - ones[i-1] - 1\n        choices = zeros_between + 1\n        result = (result * choices) % MOD\n    \n    return result\n```",
