    "global index": "AIME_44",
    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem step by step. \n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Find the number of cubic polynomials $p(x) = x^3 + ax^2 + bx + c,$ where $a, b,$ and $c$ are integers in $\\{{-20,-19,-18,\\ldots,18,19,20\\}},$ such that there is a unique integer $m \\not= 2$ with $p(m) = p(2).$\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We need cubic polynomials \\(p(x) = x^3 + a x^2 + b x + c\\) with integer coefficients from \\(-20\\) to \\(20\\) such that there is exactly one integer \\(m \\neq 2\\) with \\(p(m) = p(2)\\).\n\nLet \\(q(x) = p(x) - p(2)\\). Then \\(q(2)=0\\) and \\(q(x)\\) is a cubic with leading coefficient \\(1\\). Factor:  \n\\[\nq(x) = (x-2)(x^2 + px + q)\n\\]\nsuch that the quadratic factor has **exactly one integer root** different from \\(2\\), and that root must be the unique integer \\(m\\). This forces the quadratic to have discriminant a perfect square \\(k^2\\), giving integer roots.  \nThe only way to have exactly one integer root besides \\(2\\) is: either the quadratic has a double root \\(r \\neq 2\\), or one root equals \\(2\\) and the other is a distinct integer \\(m\\). But if \\(2\\) is already a root of the cubic, the quadratic could also have \\(2\\) as a root.\n\nIf the quadratic has a double root \\(r \\neq 2\\), then \\(q(x) = (x-2)(x-r)^2\\), giving two distinct integer inputs \\(2\\) and \\(r\\) where \\(p\\) matches; that would give exactly one *other* integer \\(m = r\\), satisfying the condition.  \nIf the quadratic has distinct roots, one being \\(2\\) and the other \\(m\\neq2\\), then \\(q(x) = (x-2)^2(x-m)\\), but then both \\(2\\) and \\(m\\) give same value, which still yields exactly one \\(m \\neq 2\\). Both cases count.\n\nSo the cubic is determined by choosing integers \\(r\\) and \\(s\\) (roots of quadratic) from \\(-20\\) to \\(20\\) such that at most one equals \\(2\\), but the condition “unique integer \\(m \\neq 2\\)” means exactly one of \\(\\{r,s\\}\\) is \\(\\neq 2\\) and the other equals \\(2\\), or both are equal to some \\(r\\neq2\\) (double root). That counts all choices where among \\(r,s\\) (order irrelevant) we have:\n- Case A: \\(r = s = t\\), \\(t \\neq 2\\) → \\(t\\) can be any of the 41 integers except 2 → 40 possibilities.\n- Case B: \\(\\{r,s\\} = \\{2, t\\}\\) with \\(t \\neq 2\\) → \\(t\\) can be any of 40 values → 40 possibilities.\n\nTotal pairs = \\(40 + 40 = 80\\).  \nGiven \\(r,s\\), we compute coefficients \\(a,b,c\\) by expanding:\n\\[\n(x-2)(x^2 + px + q) = x^3 + (p-2)x^2 + (q-2p)x - 2q,\n\\]\nwhere \\(p = -(r+s)\\), \\(q = rs\\). Since \\(r,s\\) are integers between \\(-20,20\\), \\(a,b,c\\) automatically fall in the allowed range? We must check boundaries: For \\(r=s=20\\), \\(p=-40\\), \\(q=400\\) gives \\(a = p-2 = -42\\) — out of range. So we must restrict to those \\((r,s)\\) such that all of \\(a,b,c\\) lie within \\([-20,20]\\).\n\nCompute:\n\\[\na = - (r+s) - 2, \\quad b = rs - 2(-(r+s)) = rs + 2(r+s), \\quad c = -2rs.\n\\]\nConstraints:  \n\\(-20 \\le -(r+s)-2 \\le 20 \\) → \\(-22 \\le -(r+s) \\le 18\\) → \\(-18 \\le r+s \\le 22\\).  \n\\(-20 \\le rs + 2(r+s) \\le 20\\).  \n\\(-20 \\le -2rs \\le 20\\) → \\(-10 \\le rs \\le 10\\).\n\nThe product bound is very strict: \\( |rs| \\le 10\\).  \nNow enumerate integer pairs \\((r,s)\\) from \\(-20\\) to \\(20\\) with \\(|rs| \\le 10\\) and satisfying the sum condition and case conditions above.\n\nCase A: \\(r=s=t\\), \\(t\\neq2\\). Then \\(t^2 \\le 10\\) → \\(t \\in \\{-3,-2,-1,0,1,3\\}\\) (excluding 2).  \nCheck sum condition: \\(2t\\) must be between -18 and 22; all these are fine. Possible t: \\(-3,-2,-1,0,1,3\\) → six.\n\nCase B: \\(\\{r,s\\} = \\{2,t\\}\\), \\(t\\neq2\\). Then \\(rs = 2t\\) must satisfy \\(|2t| \\le 10\\) → \\(|t| \\le 5\\). Also \\(t \\neq 2\\).  \nCheck sum: \\(r+s = 2+t\\) must be between -18 and 22; that holds for \\(-20\\le t\\le20\\), so fine.  \nPossible \\(t\\): \\(-5,-4,-3,-1,0,1,3,4,5\\) → that’s 9 values. But order doesn’t matter, each gives exactly one unordered pair \\(\\{2,t\\}\\).\n\nTotal = \\(6 + 9 = 15\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{15}\n\\]",
