    "prompt": "Please solve the following mathematical problem step by step. \n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Find the largest possible real part of \\[(75+117i)z+\\frac{{96+144i}}{{z}}\\]where $z$ is a complex number with $|z|=4$.\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format, where the content inside the braces is the exact mathematical expression or number. For example: \\boxed{{42}}. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We let \\( z = 4e^{i\\theta} \\). Then the expression becomes  \n\\[\n(75+117i)(4e^{i\\theta}) + \\frac{96+144i}{4e^{i\\theta}}\n= 4(75+117i)e^{i\\theta} + \\frac{96+144i}{4}e^{-i\\theta}.\n\\]  \nFactor appropriately: first term \\( 4(75+117i) = 300 + 468i \\), second coefficient \\( \\frac{96+144i}{4} = 24 + 36i \\).  \nNotice both coefficients are multiples: \\( 300+468i = 12(25+39i) \\) and \\( 24+36i = 12(2+3i) \\). The real part is  \n\\[\n12\\big[ (25\\cos\\theta - 39\\sin\\theta) + (2\\cos\\theta + 3\\sin\\theta) \\big] \n= 12(27\\cos\\theta - 36\\sin\\theta) = 12 \\cdot 45 \\cos(\\theta + \\phi)\n\\]  \nwith \\( \\phi = \\arctan(36/27) = \\arctan(4/3) \\).  \nMaximum real part is \\( 12 \\cdot 45 = 540 \\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{540}\n\\]",
