 J.\n\nBut J ends at -1/20 = -0.05, excludes [ -0.05, 0 ].\n\nBut is there a larger interval? D is $ [-0.5, 0] $ — all u from -0.5 to 0.\n\nAt u=0: valid  \nu=-0.5: valid  \nu=-0.4: 2u = -0.8 ≥ -1? Yes  \n-20u = 8 ≥ -1? Yes\n\nWhat about u = 0.04? Then 2u = 0.08 ≥ -1? Yes, and -20u = -0.8 ≥ -1? -0.8 ≥ -1 → true\n\nBut -0.8 is greater than -1, so -20u = -0.8 is **not less than -1**, so not in $ (-\\infty, -1) $ → valid\n\nSo actually any $ u \\in [-0.5, 0.05] $ should work\n\nBut 0.05 = 1/20\n\nSo interval is $ [-\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{20}] $\n\nBut none of the options match that.\n\nOptions:\n\nA. [-2, -1/2]  \nB. [0, 1/2]  \nC. [-1/20, 0]  \nD. [-1/2, 0]  \nE. [-1, 0]  \nF. [1/20, 1/2]  \nG. [-1,1]  \nH. [0,2]  \nI. [1,2]  \nJ. [-1/2, -1/20]\n\nNow, correct interval is $ u \\geq -1/2 $ and $ u \\leq 1/20 $\n\nSo $ u \\in [-1/2, 1/20] $\n\nNow check which of the options is a subset? But the question says \"what interval consists of all u such that...\" — so it should be **exactly** the set.\n\nBut none of the options is $ [-1/2, 1/20] $\n\nWait — option J is $ [-1/2, -1/20] $, which is $ [-0.5, -0.05] $\n\nBut for example, u = 0 is valid: 2u=0, -20u=0, both not in $ (-\\infty, -1) $, so 0 should be included.\n\nBut J excludes 0.\n\nSimilarly, D is $ [-0.5, 0] $ — includes 0, good.\n\nBut 1/20 = 0.05 > 0, so u = 0.05 is valid: 2u = 0.1 > -1? yes, -20u = -1 → is -1 in $ (-\\infty, -1) $? no, because open interval. So -1 not included, so -20u = -1 is not in $ (-\\infty, -1) $, so valid.\n\nSo u=0.05 should be included.\n\nBut D only goes to 0.\n\nSo D excludes positive u like 0.01 — but u=0.01: 2u=0.02 ≥ -1? yes, -20u = -0.2 ≥ -1? yes, since -0.2 > -1\n\nSo -20u = -0.2 > -1 → not in $ (-\\infty, -1) $? — wait: -0.2 > -1, so -0.2 is greater than -1, so -0.2 is not less than -1 → so not in $ (-\\infty, -1) $? Correct.\n\nSo for u > 0, as long as u ≤ 1/20 = 0.05\n\n-20u ≥ -1 → u ≤ 1/20\n\nSo for u in (0, 0.05], -20u in [-1, 0) → and -1 is not in $ (-\\infty, -1) $, since