    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The equation \\(|x^2 - 3| = x\\) implies \\(x \\ge 0\\), since the absolute value is nonnegative.  \nSolving gives \\(x^2 - 3 = x\\) or \\(x^2 - 3 = -x\\), leading to roots \\(x = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{13}}{2}\\) and \\(x = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{13}}{2}\\), but after applying \\(x \\ge 0\\), the valid roots are only \\(\\frac{1 + \\sqrt{13}}{2}\\) and \\(\\frac{-1 + \\sqrt{13}}{2}\\).  \nThe range (maximum minus minimum) of these two positive roots is \\(\\frac{1+\\sqrt{13}}{2} - \\frac{-1+\\sqrt{13}}{2} = 1\\), so the answer is D.  \n\n\\boxed{D}",
