    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We need to determine the bit rate from the number of quantization levels and word length.  \nWith \\( M = 16 \\), we have \\( \\log_2 16 = 4 \\) bits per sample.  \nWord length is \\( 400 \\, \\mu\\text{s} = 4 \\times 10^{-4} \\, \\text{s} \\), so bit rate \\( R_b = \\frac{4}{4 \\times 10^{-4}} = 10^4 \\) bps.  \nFor binary PCM, minimum bandwidth \\( f_c = R_b/2 = 5000 \\, \\text{Hz} \\), but the options suggest different plausible values; recalculating shows \\( 1/400\\mu s = 2500 \\) samples/s × 4 bits = 10,000 bps → \\( f_c = 5 \\, \\text{kHz} \\), not matching; noting the word length might be bit duration: if word length is 400 μs for 4 bits, bit duration = 100 μs → \\( R_b = 10,000 \\) bps → \\( f_c = 5 \\, \\text{kHz} \\). Rechecking: given numbers 2000 Hz with half bandwidth 1000 Hz → \\( R_b = 2000 \\) bps implies 2000/4 = 500 samples/s, so sample period 2 ms. This matches format: using \\( f_c = R_b/2 \\). For \\( M=16 \\) with word length = 400 μs for 4 bits, bit rate = 10,000 bps → \\( f_c = 5 \\, \\text{kHz} \\) not listed; actual matching shows choice C with 2.00 kHz means 4 kbps and with 400 μs word length could be for 1 bit?  \n\nGiven options, the computed minimum bandwidth closest to calculations works out to **2.00 × 10³ Hz for 16 levels**, and half bandwidth then reduces bits to keep bit rate same, resulting in \\( M=4 \\); hence the pair (2000 Hz, 16) for part (a) and (1000 Hz, 4) for part (b) matches **I**, but I says 2.53 kHz and 4. Correction: direct computation using formula \\( f_c = \\frac{R_b}{2} = \\frac{(\\log_2 M)/T}{2} \\) yields with \\(T=400\\mu s\\) and \\(M=16\\): \\(R_b = 4/400e-6=10^4\\), \\(f_c=5\\) kHz no match; swapping to T as bit duration: if 400 μs per bit, then \\(R_b=2500\\) bps, \\(f_c=1250\\) Hz, half gives 625 Hz → M=2 → not in list. Closest plausible from options is **I** with 2.53 kHz and 4.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
