 a-c voltage = 150 volts; 2(b) alternating current = 14 amp, direct current = 9 amp\nI. 1(a) d-c voltage = 295 volts, d-c current = 5.08 amp; 1(b) a-c input current = 7.5 amp; 2(a) a-c voltage = 140 volts; 2(b) alternating current = 17.86 amp, direct current = 11.9 amp\nJ. 1(a) d-c voltage = 330 volts, d-c current = 4.55 amp; 1(b) a-c input current = 6.35 amp; 2(a) a-c voltage = 220 volts; 2(b) alternating current = 11.36 amp, direct current = 7.58 amp\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "For question (1), with a 1.5-kW load at 230 V AC and unity power factor, the AC input current is $ I = \\frac{1500}{230} \\approx 6.52 \\, \\text{A} $, closest to 6.5 A. The DC voltage in a single-phase full-wave rectifier is approximately $ V_{dc} = 0.9 \\times V_{ac} \\times \\sqrt{2} \\approx 0.9 \\times 230 \\times 1.414 \\approx 292.5 \\, \\text{V} $, but practical designs often yield higher due to filtering; however, using $ V_{dc} \\approx \\sqrt{2} \\times 230 \\approx 325 \\, \\text{V} $ (peak) is common, and $ I_{dc} = \\frac{1500}{325} \\approx 4.62 \\, \\text{A} $. For question (2), inverting operation at 2.5 kW with 230 V DC input gives $ I_{dc} = \\frac{2500}{230} \\approx 10.87 \\, \\text{A} $, and AC voltage output consistent with conversion ratio — options with AC voltage around 162.5 V and AC current ~15.4 A align with $ 162.5 \\times 15.4 \\approx 2500 \\, \\text{W} $.\n\nThese values match option G most closely.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
