  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10863",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: From economy considerations, suggest the use of either aluminium alloy or copper alloy fins. The following data is given: Density of the aluminium alloy = 2400 kg/m^3 Density of the copper allow = 8400 kg/m^3 Conductivity of the aluminium alloy = 78 kCal/m-hr°C Conductivity of the copper alloy = 190 kCal/m-hr°C The cost of aluminium alloy is twice the cost of copper alloy.\n\nOptions: \nA. copper alloy for the core structure, with aluminium alloy as a decorative finish\nB. aluminium alloy for low-temperature applications, copper alloy for high-temperature applications\nC. copper alloy\nD. aluminium alloy, but with increased thickness to match copper alloy performance\nE. a composite material that blends both aluminium and copper alloys\nF. equal amount of both alloys\nG. copper alloy with a coating of aluminium for enhanced conductivity\nH. alternating layers of aluminium and copper alloys\nI. neither alloy\nJ. aluminium alloy\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The decision hinges on balancing thermal performance, weight, and cost. Although copper has higher thermal conductivity, aluminium is much lighter (lower density) and, despite being twice as expensive per unit mass, its lower density makes it more cost-effective per unit volume when considering material needed for the same structural coverage. Given that aluminium's conductivity is reasonably high and the cost per unit volume is favorable due to its low density, the most economical choice overall is aluminium alloy.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
